WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:58.356 --> 01:43.056 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference March 17,

01:50.776 --> 02:23.916 align:middle
2021 CHAIR POWELL.

02:23.916 --> 02:29.656 align:middle
Good afternoon.

02:29.746 --> 02:42.456 align:middle
I would like to start by noting
that it has been a full year

02:42.746 --> 02:55.066 align:middle
since the pandemic arrived
with force on our shores.

02:55.556 --> 03:04.716 align:middle
Looking back, it was clear

03:14.846 --> 03:32.766 align:middle
that addressing a fast-moving
global pandemic would be plainly

03:35.116 --> 04:11.236 align:middle
and primarily the realm
of health-care providers

04:17.316 --> 04:32.556 align:middle
and experts, and we
are grateful to them

04:33.206 --> 04:37.776 align:middle
and to all the essential workers
for their service and sacrifice.

04:37.886 --> 04:40.896 align:middle
The danger to the U.S.
economy was also clear.

04:40.896 --> 04:46.446 align:middle
Congress provided by far the
fastest and largest response

04:47.036 --> 04:49.856 align:middle
to any postwar economic
downturn,

04:49.856 --> 04:55.576 align:middle
offering fiscal support
for households, businesses,

04:55.576 --> 05:02.266 align:middle
health-care providers, and
state and local governments.

05:02.266 --> 05:08.406 align:middle
Here at the Federal Reserve, we
rapidly deployed our full range

05:08.406 --> 05:13.736 align:middle
of tools to provide
relief and stability,

05:14.296 --> 05:20.966 align:middle
to ensure that the recovery
will be as strong as possible,

05:20.966 --> 05:27.056 align:middle
and to limit lasting
damage to the economy.

05:28.056 --> 05:29.136 align:middle
We are strongly committed

05:29.136 --> 05:31.176 align:middle
to achieving the
monetary policy goals

05:31.176 --> 05:32.356 align:middle
that Congress has given us:

05:32.736 --> 05:34.766 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

05:36.676 --> 05:39.886 align:middle
The economic fallout has
been real and widespread,

05:40.686 --> 05:42.236 align:middle
but with the benefit
of perspective,

05:42.236 --> 05:45.356 align:middle
we can say that some of the very
worst economic outcomes have

05:45.356 --> 05:49.056 align:middle
been avoided by swift and
forceful action-from Congress,

05:49.056 --> 05:51.086 align:middle
from across government,
and in cities

05:51.086 --> 05:52.416 align:middle
and towns across the country.

05:53.246 --> 05:54.906 align:middle
More people held
on to their jobs,

05:55.106 --> 05:56.836 align:middle
more businesses kept
their doors open,

05:57.216 --> 06:00.586 align:middle
and more incomes were saved
as a result of these swift

06:00.586 --> 06:02.146 align:middle
and forceful policy actions.

06:03.046 --> 06:05.176 align:middle
And while we welcome these
positive developments,

06:05.276 --> 06:06.486 align:middle
no one should be complacent.

06:07.446 --> 06:10.406 align:middle
At the Fed, we will continue to
provide the economy the support

06:10.406 --> 06:12.246 align:middle
that it needs for
as long as it takes.

06:12.456 --> 06:18.216 align:middle
Today the FOMC kept
interest rates near zero

06:18.756 --> 06:21.126 align:middle
and maintained our
sizable asset purchases.

06:21.886 --> 06:24.256 align:middle
These measures, along
with our strong guidance

06:24.256 --> 06:26.266 align:middle
on interest rates and
on our balance sheet,

06:26.926 --> 06:29.256 align:middle
will ensure that monetary
policy will continue

06:29.256 --> 06:31.636 align:middle
to deliver powerful
support to the economy

06:31.956 --> 06:33.396 align:middle
until the recovery is complete.

06:34.866 --> 06:36.436 align:middle
The path of the economy
continues

06:36.436 --> 06:38.976 align:middle
to depend significantly
on the course of the virus

06:39.426 --> 06:41.696 align:middle
and the measures undertaken
to control its spread.

06:42.776 --> 06:45.726 align:middle
Since January, the number of
new cases, hospitalizations,

06:45.726 --> 06:49.516 align:middle
and deaths has fallen, and
ongoing vaccinations offer hope

06:49.636 --> 06:52.396 align:middle
for a return to more normal
conditions later this year.

06:53.106 --> 06:55.886 align:middle
In the meantime,
continued observance

06:55.886 --> 06:57.206 align:middle
of social-distancing measures

06:57.206 --> 06:59.376 align:middle
and wearing masks
will help us reach

06:59.376 --> 07:00.866 align:middle
that goal as soon as possible.

07:01.416 --> 07:05.056 align:middle
The economic recovery remains
uneven and far from complete,

07:05.306 --> 07:06.936 align:middle
and the path ahead
remains uncertain.

07:08.136 --> 07:10.706 align:middle
Following the moderation
in the pace of the recovery

07:10.706 --> 07:12.586 align:middle
that began toward
the end of last year,

07:13.336 --> 07:15.916 align:middle
indicators of economic activity
and employment have turned

07:15.916 --> 07:17.906 align:middle
up recently, although
the sectors

07:17.906 --> 07:20.666 align:middle
of the economy most adversely
affected by the resurgence

07:20.666 --> 07:23.966 align:middle
of the virus and by greater
social distancing remain weak.

07:24.986 --> 07:28.296 align:middle
Household spending on goods has
risen notably so far this year.

07:29.196 --> 07:32.156 align:middle
In contrast, household spending
on services remains low,

07:32.456 --> 07:35.116 align:middle
especially in services that
typically require people

07:35.116 --> 07:38.216 align:middle
to gather closely, including
travel and hospitality.

07:38.606 --> 07:41.626 align:middle
The housing sector has
more than fully recovered

07:41.626 --> 07:44.036 align:middle
from the downturn, while
business investment

07:44.036 --> 07:46.866 align:middle
and manufacturing production
have also picked up.

07:47.876 --> 07:50.256 align:middle
The overall recovery
in economic activity

07:50.256 --> 07:52.346 align:middle
since last spring
is due importantly

07:52.346 --> 07:55.166 align:middle
to unprecedented fiscal and
monetary policy actions,

07:55.766 --> 07:58.296 align:middle
which have provided essential
support to households,

07:58.296 --> 07:59.516 align:middle
businesses, and communities.

07:59.986 --> 08:03.286 align:middle
The recovery has progressed more
quickly than generally expected,

08:03.756 --> 08:06.146 align:middle
and forecasts from
FOMC participants

08:06.146 --> 08:09.366 align:middle
for economic growth this year
have been revised up notably

08:09.366 --> 08:12.206 align:middle
since our December Summary
of Economic Projections.

08:12.936 --> 08:14.496 align:middle
In commenting on the
stronger outlook,

08:14.616 --> 08:17.706 align:middle
participants noted progress
on vaccinations as well

08:17.706 --> 08:18.956 align:middle
as recent fiscal policy.

08:19.506 --> 08:22.396 align:middle
As with overall economic
activity,

08:22.636 --> 08:24.926 align:middle
conditions in the labor market
have turned up recently.

08:26.296 --> 08:30.496 align:middle
Employment rose by 379,000
in February, as the leisure

08:30.496 --> 08:33.716 align:middle
and hospitality sector recouped
about two-thirds of the jobs

08:33.716 --> 08:35.416 align:middle
that were lost in
December and January.

08:37.566 --> 08:40.206 align:middle
Nonetheless, employment
in this sector is more

08:40.206 --> 08:43.016 align:middle
than 3 million below its level
at the onset of the pandemic.

08:43.996 --> 08:45.206 align:middle
For the economy as a whole,

08:45.276 --> 08:49.006 align:middle
employment is 9.5 million
below its pre-pandemic level.

08:50.046 --> 08:53.136 align:middle
The unemployment rate remains
elevated at 6.2 percent

08:53.136 --> 08:56.466 align:middle
in February; this figure
understates the shortfall

08:56.466 --> 08:59.226 align:middle
in employment, particularly
as participation

08:59.226 --> 09:01.576 align:middle
in the labor market
remains notably below

09:01.786 --> 09:02.936 align:middle
pre-pandemic levels.

09:04.506 --> 09:07.896 align:middle
Looking ahead, FOMC participants
project the unemployment rate

09:07.896 --> 09:12.406 align:middle
to continue to decline; the
median projection is 4.5 percent

09:12.406 --> 09:14.186 align:middle
at the end of this
year and moves

09:14.186 --> 09:17.966 align:middle
down to 3.5 percent
by the end of 2023.

09:19.116 --> 09:22.566 align:middle
The economic downturn has not
fallen equally on all Americans,

09:22.566 --> 09:24.826 align:middle
and those least able to shoulder
the burden have been the

09:24.826 --> 09:25.496 align:middle
hardest hit.

09:26.296 --> 09:27.686 align:middle
In particular, the high level

09:27.686 --> 09:29.856 align:middle
of joblessness has
been especially severe

09:30.206 --> 09:32.436 align:middle
for lower-wage workers
in the service sector

09:32.666 --> 09:34.566 align:middle
and for African Americans
and Hispanics.

09:34.936 --> 09:38.246 align:middle
The economic dislocation
has upended many lives

09:38.246 --> 09:42.226 align:middle
and created great
uncertainty about the future.

09:42.376 --> 09:44.746 align:middle
Overall inflation remains
below our 2 percent

09:44.746 --> 09:45.626 align:middle
longer-run objective.

09:46.626 --> 09:48.886 align:middle
Over the next few
months, 12-month measures

09:48.886 --> 09:52.076 align:middle
of inflation will move up as
the very low readings from March

09:52.076 --> 09:54.866 align:middle
and April of last year fall
out of the calculation.

09:56.086 --> 09:58.996 align:middle
Beyond these base effects, we
could also see upward pressure

09:58.996 --> 10:02.626 align:middle
on prices if spending rebounds
quickly as the economy continues

10:02.626 --> 10:04.776 align:middle
to reopen, particularly

10:04.776 --> 10:08.576 align:middle
if supply bottlenecks limit how
quickly production can respond

10:08.576 --> 10:09.376 align:middle
in the near term.

10:10.996 --> 10:15.606 align:middle
However, these one-time
increases in prices are likely

10:15.606 --> 10:17.926 align:middle
to have only transient
effects on inflation.

10:19.256 --> 10:20.726 align:middle
The median inflation projection

10:20.726 --> 10:24.456 align:middle
of FOMC participants is 2.4
percent this year and declines

10:24.456 --> 10:26.516 align:middle
to 2 percent next year
before moving back

10:26.516 --> 10:28.466 align:middle
up by the end of 2023.

10:29.016 --> 10:32.636 align:middle
The Fed's response to this
crisis has been guided

10:32.636 --> 10:34.956 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

10:34.956 --> 10:36.776 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people,

10:37.186 --> 10:38.646 align:middle
along with our responsibilities

10:38.646 --> 10:40.436 align:middle
to promote the financial-the
stability

10:40.436 --> 10:41.406 align:middle
of the financial system.

10:42.386 --> 10:44.686 align:middle
As we say in our Statement
on Longer-Run Goals

10:44.686 --> 10:48.346 align:middle
and Monetary Policy Strategy,
we view maximum employment as a

10:48.346 --> 10:50.246 align:middle
"broad-based and
inclusive goal."

10:50.976 --> 10:53.326 align:middle
Our ability to achieve
maximum employment

10:53.326 --> 10:55.336 align:middle
in the years ahead
depends importantly

10:55.336 --> 10:59.056 align:middle
on having longer-term inflation
expectations well anchored

10:59.056 --> 10:59.906 align:middle
at 2 percent.

11:00.306 --> 11:04.756 align:middle
As the Committee reiterated
in today's policy statement,

11:04.876 --> 11:07.426 align:middle
with inflation running
persistently below 2 percent,

11:07.996 --> 11:11.056 align:middle
we will aim to achieve inflation
moderately above 2 percent

11:11.056 --> 11:14.856 align:middle
for some time so that inflation
averages 2 percent over time

11:15.416 --> 11:18.076 align:middle
and longer-term inflation
expectations remain well

11:18.076 --> 11:19.256 align:middle
anchored at 2 percent.

11:20.336 --> 11:22.516 align:middle
We expect to maintain
an accommodative stance

11:22.516 --> 11:24.516 align:middle
of monetary policy
until these employment

11:24.516 --> 11:26.356 align:middle
and inflation outcomes
are achieved.

11:26.846 --> 11:28.736 align:middle
With regard to interest rates,

11:28.946 --> 11:30.946 align:middle
we continue to expect
it will be appropriate

11:30.946 --> 11:34.366 align:middle
to maintain the current 0
to ¼ percent target range

11:34.366 --> 11:35.426 align:middle
for the federal funds rate

11:35.986 --> 11:38.586 align:middle
until labor market conditions
have reached levels consistent

11:38.586 --> 11:40.566 align:middle
with the Committee's
assessment of maximum employment

11:41.406 --> 11:43.986 align:middle
and inflation has risen to
2 percent and is on track

11:43.986 --> 11:46.596 align:middle
to moderately exceed 2
percent for some time.

11:47.626 --> 11:49.996 align:middle
I would note that a
transitory rise in inflation

11:50.036 --> 11:53.266 align:middle
above 2 percent, as seems
likely to occur this year,

11:53.856 --> 11:55.036 align:middle
would not meet this standard.

11:56.026 --> 11:58.526 align:middle
In addition, we will continue
to increase our holdings

11:58.526 --> 12:01.626 align:middle
of Treasury securities by at
least $80 billion per month

12:02.296 --> 12:04.226 align:middle
and of agency mortgage-backed
securities

12:04.226 --> 12:06.176 align:middle
by at least $40 billion
per month

12:07.056 --> 12:09.786 align:middle
until substantial further
progress has been made toward

12:09.836 --> 12:12.416 align:middle
our maximum-employment
and price-stability goals.

12:12.966 --> 12:14.746 align:middle
The increase in our
balance sheet

12:14.746 --> 12:17.846 align:middle
since last March has materially
eased financial conditions

12:18.586 --> 12:20.976 align:middle
and is providing substantial
support to the economy.

12:22.006 --> 12:24.306 align:middle
The economy is a long
way from our employment

12:24.306 --> 12:27.056 align:middle
and inflation goals, and it
is likely to take some time

12:27.166 --> 12:29.576 align:middle
for substantial further
progress to be achieved.

12:30.006 --> 12:33.286 align:middle
Our forward guidance for
the federal funds rate,

12:33.456 --> 12:35.006 align:middle
along with our balance
sheet guidance,

12:35.316 --> 12:37.166 align:middle
will ensure that the stance

12:37.166 --> 12:39.406 align:middle
of monetary policy remains
highly accommodative

12:39.606 --> 12:40.976 align:middle
as the recovery progresses.

12:42.016 --> 12:44.776 align:middle
Our guidance is outcome
based and ties the path

12:44.776 --> 12:46.596 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
and the balance sheet

12:47.126 --> 12:50.496 align:middle
to progress toward reaching our
employment and inflation goals.

12:51.766 --> 12:53.506 align:middle
Overall, our interest rate

12:53.506 --> 12:56.146 align:middle
and balance sheet tools are
providing powerful support

12:56.146 --> 12:59.806 align:middle
to the economy and
will continue to do so.

13:00.046 --> 13:03.396 align:middle
To conclude, we understand that
our actions affect communities,

13:03.446 --> 13:05.476 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

13:06.336 --> 13:09.166 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

13:10.176 --> 13:12.226 align:middle
We are committed to using
our full range of tools

13:12.226 --> 13:14.996 align:middle
to support the economy and to
help assure that the recovery

13:14.996 --> 13:18.426 align:middle
from this difficult period
will be as robust as possible.

13:19.556 --> 13:19.956 align:middle
Thank you.

13:20.096 --> 13:23.946 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

13:26.316 --> 13:27.446 align:middle
JOE PAVEL.

13:32.036 --> 13:33.326 align:middle
Howard from Reuters.

13:34.616 --> 13:35.166 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

13:35.166 --> 13:36.936 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell, and,
and thanks for that.

13:36.936 --> 13:43.176 align:middle
So could you talk us through how
the, the forecasts for 2021 map

13:43.176 --> 13:46.376 align:middle
into the "substantial further
progress" definition-you know,

13:46.376 --> 13:48.406 align:middle
2.4 percent inflation-I
understand that's

13:48.406 --> 13:49.616 align:middle
considered transitory.

13:50.126 --> 13:51.996 align:middle
That still seems like
some progress there,

13:52.066 --> 13:54.076 align:middle
4½ percent unemployment.

13:54.166 --> 13:56.446 align:middle
Is it time to start talking

13:56.446 --> 13:58.866 align:middle
about talking about
tapering yet?

14:00.366 --> 14:00.866 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:01.226 --> 14:01.686 align:middle
[Laughter] Not yet.

14:01.966 --> 14:05.056 align:middle
So, as you-as you pointed out,

14:05.056 --> 14:08.326 align:middle
we've said that we would
continue asset purchases

14:08.326 --> 14:12.206 align:middle
at this pace until we see
substantial further progress.

14:12.206 --> 14:14.886 align:middle
And that's actual progress,
not forecast progress.

14:15.246 --> 14:18.846 align:middle
So-and that's a difference
from, from our past approach.

14:18.846 --> 14:22.066 align:middle
So-and what we mean by that
is, is pretty straightforward.

14:22.066 --> 14:23.116 align:middle
It is, we want to see that,

14:23.536 --> 14:26.636 align:middle
that the labor markets have
moved-labor market conditions

14:26.636 --> 14:30.986 align:middle
have moved-you know, have made
substantial progress toward

14:31.076 --> 14:31.976 align:middle
maximum employment,

14:31.976 --> 14:35.526 align:middle
and inflation has made
substantial progress toward the

14:35.526 --> 14:36.516 align:middle
2 percent goal.

14:36.516 --> 14:37.586 align:middle
That's what we're
going to want to see.

14:37.636 --> 14:40.416 align:middle
Now, that obviously includes
an element of judgment.

14:41.116 --> 14:43.756 align:middle
And when we see-we'll be-we'll
be carefully looking ahead.

14:43.756 --> 14:48.076 align:middle
We, we also understand that
we, we will want to provide

14:48.446 --> 14:51.416 align:middle
as much advance notice of any
potential taper as possible.

14:51.416 --> 14:53.056 align:middle
So when we see that
we're on track,

14:53.446 --> 14:55.876 align:middle
when we see actual data coming
in that suggests that we're

14:55.876 --> 14:59.316 align:middle
on track to perhaps achieve
substantial further progress,

15:00.066 --> 15:00.656 align:middle
then we'll say so.

15:00.696 --> 15:02.596 align:middle
And we'll say so well in advance

15:02.596 --> 15:04.686 align:middle
of any decision to
actually taper.

15:05.756 --> 15:06.196 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

15:06.196 --> 15:07.556 align:middle
If, if I could follow
up on that,

15:07.556 --> 15:10.036 align:middle
this shift in the
dots-why wouldn't

15:10.036 --> 15:13.166 align:middle
that suggest a weakening
of the commitment here?

15:13.166 --> 15:17.226 align:middle
An awful lot of people
shifted into 2022, it seems.

15:17.226 --> 15:18.016 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:18.016 --> 15:19.176 align:middle
I, I don't see that at all.

15:19.176 --> 15:20.586 align:middle
You know, we, we have a, a range

15:20.586 --> 15:22.676 align:middle
of perspectives on
the Committee.

15:22.676 --> 15:24.026 align:middle
I welcome that.

15:24.906 --> 15:27.766 align:middle
And, you know, we, we
have-we debate things,

15:27.766 --> 15:29.546 align:middle
we discuss things, and
we always come together

15:29.546 --> 15:31.776 align:middle
around a-around a, a solution.

15:31.776 --> 15:36.336 align:middle
But, you know, the, the strong
bulk of the Committee is,

15:36.746 --> 15:41.596 align:middle
is not showing a rate increase
during this forecast period.

15:42.226 --> 15:45.006 align:middle
And, you know, as,
as data improve,

15:45.006 --> 15:47.466 align:middle
as the outlook improves
very significantly

15:47.466 --> 15:48.596 align:middle
since the December meeting,

15:48.816 --> 15:51.026 align:middle
you would expect
forecasts to move up.

15:51.376 --> 15:53.966 align:middle
It's probably not a surprise
that some people would bring

15:53.966 --> 15:56.326 align:middle
in their estimate of the
appropriate time for liftoff.

15:56.626 --> 15:59.616 align:middle
Nonetheless, you know, the
bulk of the Committee-the,

15:59.746 --> 16:01.706 align:middle
the largest part by far
of the Committee is,

16:01.706 --> 16:04.476 align:middle
is-doesn't show a rate
increase during this period.

16:04.476 --> 16:06.856 align:middle
And, again, part
of that is wanting

16:06.856 --> 16:10.826 align:middle
to see actual data rather than
just a forecast at this point.

16:10.826 --> 16:15.026 align:middle
We do expect that we'll
begin to make faster progress

16:15.236 --> 16:19.086 align:middle
on both spending-you know,
labor markets and inflation

16:19.526 --> 16:22.436 align:middle
as the year goes on because of
the progress with the vaccines,

16:22.436 --> 16:24.706 align:middle
because of the fiscal
support that we're getting.

16:25.006 --> 16:26.016 align:middle
We expect that to happen.

16:26.016 --> 16:27.476 align:middle
But, you know, we'll
have to see it first.

16:27.906 --> 16:30.986 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:30.986 --> 16:31.706 align:middle
Great, thank you.

16:31.706 --> 16:35.266 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

16:35.266 --> 16:36.096 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

16:36.096 --> 16:38.816 align:middle
I wanted to ask about the
supplementary leverage ratio.

16:38.816 --> 16:40.396 align:middle
You know, there's
been a lot of talk

16:40.396 --> 16:44.566 align:middle
about what you all are going to
do this month, which-I'm happy

16:44.566 --> 16:45.816 align:middle
to hear an update
if you have one.

16:45.816 --> 16:49.256 align:middle
But just sort of more broadly,
do you think, long term,

16:49.496 --> 16:52.646 align:middle
that the leverage
ratio poses problems

16:52.846 --> 16:55.596 align:middle
for implementing
monetary policy at a time

16:55.596 --> 16:57.996 align:middle
when the reserve supply
is going to remain large?

16:57.996 --> 17:01.906 align:middle
And, if so, do you think the
changes to the leverage ratio,

17:01.906 --> 17:04.276 align:middle
including the SLR, are the
way to deal with that problem?

17:05.106 --> 17:05.596 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:05.596 --> 17:08.316 align:middle
Victoria, we'll have
something to announce

17:08.316 --> 17:09.586 align:middle
on that in coming days.

17:10.526 --> 17:13.616 align:middle
And I'm not going to
expound upon your questions.

17:13.616 --> 17:15.366 align:middle
Why don't you-why don't
you ask another question

17:15.366 --> 17:15.996 align:middle
if you'd like to?

17:16.086 --> 17:17.846 align:middle
Because, because that
one-I'm just going to say

17:17.846 --> 17:19.216 align:middle
that we'll have something
in coming days.

17:20.216 --> 17:20.926 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

17:20.926 --> 17:22.246 align:middle
Sure. Okay.

17:22.246 --> 17:24.846 align:middle
Well, then I'll ask
about unemployment.

17:24.846 --> 17:28.236 align:middle
You know, there's-the
unemployment rate is-you all

17:28.236 --> 17:31.336 align:middle
have projections for the U-6
rate, but you've also been,

17:31.626 --> 17:33.886 align:middle
you know, really
emphasizing the fact

17:33.886 --> 17:35.876 align:middle
that that's not the only thing
that you all are looking at.

17:35.876 --> 17:37.646 align:middle
You're also looking at
labor force participation

17:37.646 --> 17:38.476 align:middle
and things like that.

17:38.476 --> 17:41.876 align:middle
So are you all looking
at ways of maybe adding

17:41.876 --> 17:45.046 align:middle
to how you're projecting the
unemployment rate to the Summary

17:45.046 --> 17:46.036 align:middle
of Economic Projections?

17:46.236 --> 17:47.276 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:47.456 --> 17:50.906 align:middle
Well, let me say, as
we say in our Statement

17:50.906 --> 17:53.056 align:middle
on Longer-Run Goals and
Monetary Policy Strategy,

17:53.056 --> 17:55.696 align:middle
we look to a range of
indicators on the labor market.

17:55.696 --> 17:58.416 align:middle
We never only looked at
the unemployment rate,

17:58.416 --> 18:00.336 align:middle
which is the only indicator of,

18:00.336 --> 18:03.166 align:middle
of labor market outcomes
that's in the SEP.

18:03.166 --> 18:05.576 align:middle
We look at a very broad range.

18:05.576 --> 18:07.816 align:middle
You hear us talk all the
time about participation;

18:07.936 --> 18:10.426 align:middle
about employment to population,
which is the combination

18:10.426 --> 18:14.086 align:middle
of the two; about different
measures of, of unemployment.

18:14.086 --> 18:18.626 align:middle
So it's wages, it's,
it's the job flows, it's,

18:18.626 --> 18:20.126 align:middle
you know, all of those things.

18:20.266 --> 18:23.426 align:middle
They go into an
assessment-disparities

18:23.426 --> 18:24.086 align:middle
of various groups.

18:24.206 --> 18:26.726 align:middle
All that goes into an assessment
of maximum employment.

18:27.546 --> 18:30.546 align:middle
The, the-trying to incorporate
all of that into the Summary

18:30.546 --> 18:32.586 align:middle
of Economic Projections
would not be practical.

18:33.106 --> 18:35.056 align:middle
You know, obviously, the thing

18:35.056 --> 18:36.916 align:middle
that we do include is just
the unemployment rate,

18:36.916 --> 18:39.946 align:middle
and that's a very
insufficient statistic.

18:39.946 --> 18:42.176 align:middle
So-it, it doesn't include
a lot of other things

18:42.666 --> 18:43.866 align:middle
that we-that we do look at.

18:44.016 --> 18:47.036 align:middle
And I, I wouldn't want
to say that we're looking

18:47.036 --> 18:48.706 align:middle
to include the other
dozen things

18:48.706 --> 18:51.156 align:middle
that we look at into the SEP.

18:51.156 --> 18:53.426 align:middle
But at time-from time
to time, we do look at,

18:53.426 --> 18:54.726 align:middle
at adding different things.

18:54.726 --> 18:58.576 align:middle
But I, I would just say
the SEP is, it's a summary.

18:58.576 --> 18:59.806 align:middle
It's one device.

19:00.176 --> 19:02.726 align:middle
It's not going to include all
of the things that we look at.

19:02.726 --> 19:04.446 align:middle
I think you know the
things that we look at-we,

19:04.446 --> 19:07.006 align:middle
we talk about them all the time.

19:07.326 --> 19:09.266 align:middle
So we're not actually
looking actively

19:09.596 --> 19:11.736 align:middle
at significantly
broadening those indicators

19:11.736 --> 19:12.626 align:middle
in the SEP right now.

19:13.086 --> 19:15.666 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:16.156 --> 19:16.726 align:middle
Thank you.

19:16.726 --> 19:19.536 align:middle
Chris Rugaber, Associated Press.

19:21.956 --> 19:22.976 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

19:23.126 --> 19:23.856 align:middle
Thank you.

19:24.536 --> 19:29.086 align:middle
Chair Powell, I wanted to ask
about the forecast overall.

19:29.266 --> 19:33.426 align:middle
You're forecasting a very low
unemployment rate next year

19:33.426 --> 19:36.546 align:middle
and in 2023, you
have inflation-or,

19:36.546 --> 19:40.066 align:middle
the Fed overall is, in the
SEP, forecasting inflation at

19:40.066 --> 19:47.366 align:middle
or above 2 percent by 2023,
yet no rate hike in any

19:47.366 --> 19:50.246 align:middle
of this-in any of
this forecast horizon.

19:50.246 --> 19:53.146 align:middle
So is this telling us that we
could see a higher inflation

19:53.146 --> 19:54.736 align:middle
rate than was projected?

19:54.736 --> 19:58.946 align:middle
Or do you not-as you've
been talking about,

19:58.946 --> 20:00.786 align:middle
is the unemployment
rate insufficient?

20:00.786 --> 20:03.886 align:middle
Or what is this telling us about
the Fed's reaction function,

20:03.886 --> 20:08.806 align:middle
that it seems you're meeting the
Fed's dual mandate by 2023, yet,

20:08.806 --> 20:10.406 align:middle
again, no rate hike expected?

20:10.836 --> 20:11.996 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:12.076 --> 20:14.386 align:middle
So I guess the first
thing to say is that the,

20:14.386 --> 20:17.506 align:middle
the SEP is not a
Committee forecast.

20:17.506 --> 20:20.586 align:middle
It's, it's not something we sit
around and debate and discuss

20:20.586 --> 20:24.026 align:middle
and approve, and say, "This
represents our, you know,

20:24.026 --> 20:26.966 align:middle
our reaction function
as a Committee."

20:26.966 --> 20:29.176 align:middle
It's a compilation
of projections

20:29.176 --> 20:30.866 align:middle
from different people.

20:30.866 --> 20:34.076 align:middle
And I-since we don't debate it
or discuss it, it would be hard

20:34.076 --> 20:37.936 align:middle
for me to say why-exactly why
each participant did what they

20:37.936 --> 20:38.856 align:middle
were going to do.

20:39.266 --> 20:46.536 align:middle
So all I would say about
this is that we've-we laid

20:46.536 --> 20:50.096 align:middle
out what I think is very
clear guidance on liftoff.

20:50.096 --> 20:52.586 align:middle
And it's really three things:
labor market conditions

20:52.586 --> 20:53.846 align:middle
that are consistent
with our estimates

20:53.846 --> 20:55.846 align:middle
of maximum employment-and,
as I mentioned,

20:55.846 --> 20:57.666 align:middle
we consider a wide
range of indicators

20:58.036 --> 20:59.616 align:middle
in assessing labor
market conditions,

20:59.876 --> 21:00.996 align:middle
not just the unemployment rate;

21:01.166 --> 21:03.396 align:middle
inflation that has reached
2 percent, and not just

21:03.396 --> 21:06.016 align:middle
on a transitory basis; and
inflation that's on track

21:06.016 --> 21:08.256 align:middle
to run moderately above
2 percent for some time.

21:08.496 --> 21:11.706 align:middle
The first two of those three
are very much data based;

21:11.706 --> 21:14.506 align:middle
the third does have a little
bit of a-of an element

21:14.506 --> 21:16.096 align:middle
of expectations in it.

21:16.646 --> 21:19.206 align:middle
So we are very much determined

21:19.206 --> 21:21.246 align:middle
to implement this
guidance in a robust way.

21:21.246 --> 21:24.086 align:middle
It is the guidance
that we chose carefully

21:24.086 --> 21:26.386 align:middle
to implement our new framework.

21:26.916 --> 21:28.916 align:middle
And to meet these standards,

21:29.026 --> 21:32.476 align:middle
we'll need to see
data, as I mentioned.

21:32.906 --> 21:36.086 align:middle
So what does-what does
this, this SEP really say?

21:36.316 --> 21:37.966 align:middle
It says that we're
committed to our framework

21:37.966 --> 21:40.036 align:middle
and to the guidance
we've provided

21:40.036 --> 21:41.116 align:middle
to implement that framework.

21:41.856 --> 21:45.226 align:middle
We will wait until the
requirements set forth

21:45.226 --> 21:47.726 align:middle
in that guidance are clearly
met before considering a change

21:47.726 --> 21:48.606 align:middle
in our policy rate.

21:49.386 --> 21:52.786 align:middle
And the last thing I'll say is
this: The state of the economy

21:52.786 --> 21:57.036 align:middle
in two or three years is highly
uncertain, and I wouldn't want

21:57.036 --> 21:58.896 align:middle
to focus too much
on the exact timing

21:58.896 --> 22:02.536 align:middle
of a potential rate increase
that far into the future.

22:03.716 --> 22:06.046 align:middle
So that's how I would
think about the, the SEP.

22:06.046 --> 22:06.666 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:07.136 --> 22:09.816 align:middle
Thank you.

22:09.986 --> 22:11.916 align:middle
Paul Kiernan.

22:11.916 --> 22:12.736 align:middle
PAUL KIERNAN.

22:15.196 --> 22:16.236 align:middle
Thank you, Chairman Powell.

22:17.236 --> 22:19.436 align:middle
My question is twofold.

22:19.646 --> 22:23.216 align:middle
One, how high are you
comfortable letting

22:23.216 --> 22:24.346 align:middle
inflation rise?

22:24.346 --> 22:27.766 align:middle
There, there is some ambiguity
in, in your new target,

22:27.766 --> 22:30.166 align:middle
as you mentioned,
"expectations driven."

22:30.726 --> 22:35.946 align:middle
And, and do you think that that
ambiguity might cause markets

22:35.946 --> 22:38.586 align:middle
to price in a lower
tolerance for inflation

22:39.046 --> 22:40.456 align:middle
than the Fed actually has,

22:40.456 --> 22:42.666 align:middle
thereby causing financial
conditions

22:42.666 --> 22:43.976 align:middle
to tighten prematurely?

22:43.976 --> 22:44.976 align:middle
Is that a concern?

22:44.976 --> 22:47.096 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

22:47.286 --> 22:51.566 align:middle
So we've said we'd like to
see inflation run moderately

22:51.566 --> 22:53.186 align:middle
above 2 percent for some time.

22:54.076 --> 22:59.496 align:middle
And we've resisted, basically,
generally, the temptation

22:59.496 --> 23:00.736 align:middle
to try to quantify that.

23:01.376 --> 23:05.636 align:middle
Part of that just is, talking
about inflation is one thing;

23:05.956 --> 23:07.486 align:middle
actually having inflation run

23:07.486 --> 23:09.526 align:middle
above 2 percent is
the real thing.

23:10.106 --> 23:14.196 align:middle
So I-you know, over the
years we've, we've talked

23:14.196 --> 23:15.576 align:middle
about 2 percent inflation
as a goal,

23:15.576 --> 23:16.536 align:middle
but we haven't achieved it.

23:16.536 --> 23:19.856 align:middle
So I, I would say we'd
like to, you know, perform.

23:20.746 --> 23:22.516 align:middle
That's what we'd
really like to do,

23:22.516 --> 23:25.066 align:middle
is to get inflation
moderately above 2 percent.

23:25.256 --> 23:27.966 align:middle
I don't want to be too
specific about what that means,

23:27.966 --> 23:29.946 align:middle
because I think it's
hard to do that.

23:30.376 --> 23:31.406 align:middle
And we haven't done it yet.

23:31.746 --> 23:37.076 align:middle
You know, when we're actually
above 2 percent, we can do that.

23:37.076 --> 23:38.276 align:middle
I-look, I, I would say this.

23:38.276 --> 23:44.556 align:middle
The fundamental change in,
in our framework is that we,

23:45.016 --> 23:47.016 align:middle
we're not going to
act preemptively based

23:47.016 --> 23:49.776 align:middle
on forecasts for the most part.

23:49.776 --> 23:51.866 align:middle
And we're going to wait
to see actual data.

23:51.866 --> 23:56.146 align:middle
And I think it will take people
time to, to adjust to that-and

23:56.146 --> 23:57.306 align:middle
to adjust to that new practice.

23:57.306 --> 23:59.356 align:middle
And the only way we can
really build the credibility

23:59.356 --> 24:00.496 align:middle
of that is by doing it.

24:01.366 --> 24:03.636 align:middle
So that's how I would
think about that.

24:03.636 --> 24:04.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:06.846 --> 24:07.766 align:middle
Thank you.

24:08.146 --> 24:09.236 align:middle
Matthew Boesler.

24:09.276 --> 24:11.166 align:middle
MATTHEW BOESLER.

24:11.166 --> 24:11.936 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

24:11.936 --> 24:13.976 align:middle
This is Matthew Boesler
with Bloomberg News.

24:13.976 --> 24:17.126 align:middle
So there's a widespread
presumption at this point

24:17.126 --> 24:19.996 align:middle
that the U.S. will reach herd
immunity sometime this year.

24:20.286 --> 24:23.516 align:middle
And all along, you've said that
the path of the economy is going

24:23.516 --> 24:25.616 align:middle
to be determined by the
course of the virus.

24:25.976 --> 24:28.316 align:middle
I'm curious, based
on the projections

24:28.316 --> 24:32.366 align:middle
that you released today that
show unemployment will still be

24:32.366 --> 24:35.746 align:middle
above estimates of maximum
employment through the end

24:35.746 --> 24:39.536 align:middle
of next year and perhaps
sometime into 2023,

24:39.806 --> 24:44.026 align:middle
do you think policymakers need
to be doing more here to sort

24:44.096 --> 24:46.126 align:middle
of align the herd immunity

24:46.126 --> 24:48.546 align:middle
and full employment
timelines, if you will?

24:48.546 --> 24:49.026 align:middle
Thank you.

24:49.666 --> 24:50.106 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:50.106 --> 24:52.406 align:middle
So, on, on herd immunity,
I, I'm really going to leave

24:52.406 --> 24:54.306 align:middle
that question to the experts.

24:54.556 --> 24:56.096 align:middle
We don't control that.

24:56.096 --> 24:59.336 align:middle
We're not responsible
for defining it.

24:59.336 --> 25:04.766 align:middle
And we'll, we'll leave that
whole discussion to the experts.

25:04.766 --> 25:07.986 align:middle
I mean, we're-what
we're focused on is,

25:08.626 --> 25:10.916 align:middle
is the part that we
control, which is the support

25:10.916 --> 25:12.106 align:middle
that we provide to the economy.

25:12.596 --> 25:14.606 align:middle
And there we've provided
very clear guidance.

25:15.176 --> 25:17.616 align:middle
And in the case of
asset purchases,

25:17.616 --> 25:20.126 align:middle
it's at their current level

25:20.316 --> 25:22.716 align:middle
until we make substantial
further progress.

25:23.036 --> 25:24.916 align:middle
There is an element of
judgment in that, and we'll,

25:25.016 --> 25:27.976 align:middle
we'll-therefore, we'll supply
clear communication well

25:27.976 --> 25:30.266 align:middle
in advance of, of
actually tapering,

25:30.266 --> 25:33.196 align:middle
and we just went through,
you know, the, the criteria

25:33.196 --> 25:35.306 align:middle
for raising interest rates.

25:35.336 --> 25:36.426 align:middle
They're very specific.

25:36.966 --> 25:40.496 align:middle
And, you know, we're,
we're very much committed

25:40.496 --> 25:42.356 align:middle
to having them fulfilled
robustly.

25:42.706 --> 25:45.276 align:middle
I, I would agree
with you that the,

25:45.736 --> 25:48.036 align:middle
the path of the virus
continues to be very important.

25:48.036 --> 25:51.636 align:middle
We, we have these, you know,
new strains with-which,

25:51.636 --> 25:53.266 align:middle
which can be quite virulent.

25:54.236 --> 25:56.236 align:middle
And we're not actually done yet.

25:56.236 --> 25:59.786 align:middle
And I, I-we're clearly
on a good path with,

25:59.786 --> 26:03.326 align:middle
with cases coming down, as I
mentioned, but we're not done.

26:03.326 --> 26:05.946 align:middle
And I'd hate to see us take
our eye off the ball before we

26:05.946 --> 26:08.576 align:middle
actually finish the job.

26:08.756 --> 26:11.356 align:middle
So that's how I would
look at that.

26:11.426 --> 26:12.276 align:middle
MATTHEW BOESLER.

26:12.596 --> 26:16.306 align:middle
If I could just briefly
follow up, how do you see sort

26:16.306 --> 26:20.246 align:middle
of the disconnect in terms of
an economy that is expected

26:20.246 --> 26:21.816 align:middle
to be widely reopened this year

26:21.816 --> 26:24.996 align:middle
but full employment
taking longer to achieve?

26:24.996 --> 26:27.866 align:middle
Is it, is it the case
that factors related

26:27.986 --> 26:32.556 align:middle
to the virus will still be
with us over the coming years?

26:32.556 --> 26:35.826 align:middle
Is that how to interpret
the forecasts?

26:35.826 --> 26:36.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:36.076 --> 26:37.196 align:middle
I think there's some of that.

26:37.196 --> 26:38.276 align:middle
Sure, there'll be some of that.

26:38.276 --> 26:39.876 align:middle
There will still be
some social distancing.

26:39.876 --> 26:44.996 align:middle
People may be, for example,
going into spaces that, that,

26:44.996 --> 26:48.306 align:middle
you know, that involve
close contact with others.

26:48.306 --> 26:49.486 align:middle
Some people will
do that right away.

26:49.486 --> 26:50.496 align:middle
Others will hold back.

26:50.926 --> 26:52.746 align:middle
And so I think there'll-there'll
be some of that.

26:53.316 --> 26:54.406 align:middle
In addition, though, remember,

26:54.406 --> 26:56.546 align:middle
there are-there are 10
million people-in the range

26:56.546 --> 26:58.846 align:middle
of 10 million people who
need to get back to work.

26:59.356 --> 27:01.506 align:middle
And it's going to take some
time for that to happen.

27:01.716 --> 27:05.676 align:middle
You know, it can-it can happen
maybe, maybe more quickly

27:05.676 --> 27:08.816 align:middle
than it has in the past, because
it involves the reopening

27:08.816 --> 27:09.996 align:middle
of a sector of the economy,

27:09.996 --> 27:12.556 align:middle
as opposed to stimulating
aggregate demand and waiting

27:12.556 --> 27:14.906 align:middle
for that to produce
job demand for workers.

27:14.906 --> 27:16.746 align:middle
This could be a different
sort of a process.

27:17.016 --> 27:17.866 align:middle
And it could be quicker.

27:17.866 --> 27:18.676 align:middle
We don't know that.

27:19.256 --> 27:21.966 align:middle
But it's, it's just
a lot of people

27:22.016 --> 27:23.846 align:middle
who have-who need
to get back to work.

27:23.846 --> 27:25.246 align:middle
And it's not going
to happen overnight.

27:25.246 --> 27:27.376 align:middle
It would be-it's going
to take some time.

27:27.566 --> 27:30.656 align:middle
No matter how well
the economy performs,

27:31.216 --> 27:35.296 align:middle
unemployment will take quite
a time to go-to go down,

27:35.296 --> 27:36.506 align:middle
and so will participation.

27:36.946 --> 27:39.496 align:middle
So that's all I can say.

27:39.496 --> 27:40.856 align:middle
I think the faster the better;

27:40.856 --> 27:42.716 align:middle
we'd love to see it come
sooner rather than later.

27:42.716 --> 27:44.176 align:middle
We'd welcome nothing
more than that.

27:44.246 --> 27:46.306 align:middle
But, realistically,
given the numbers,

27:46.836 --> 27:47.816 align:middle
it's going to take some time.

27:47.816 --> 27:48.426 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:50.456 --> 27:51.826 align:middle
Thank you, Steve Liesman.

27:51.826 --> 27:52.346 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

27:52.516 --> 27:54.706 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, thank you.

27:54.706 --> 27:56.516 align:middle
I wonder if you could-kind

27:56.516 --> 27:58.976 align:middle
of a three-parter
here, but all related.

27:59.286 --> 28:03.076 align:middle
Would you comment on the current
level of the 10-year yield

28:03.076 --> 28:04.736 align:middle
and some other long
rates out there, whether

28:04.736 --> 28:06.776 align:middle
or not you think they would
have a negative effect

28:06.776 --> 28:07.636 align:middle
on the economy?

28:07.636 --> 28:10.496 align:middle
And, if not, is there a level
that would give you concern?

28:10.496 --> 28:12.126 align:middle
And, finally, the third part:

28:12.126 --> 28:14.106 align:middle
Other central bankers
have expressed concern

28:14.106 --> 28:16.666 align:middle
about what's happened to
yields in their countries

28:16.666 --> 28:18.846 align:middle
and even some taken
action, but not you.

28:19.176 --> 28:21.566 align:middle
Could you give us
your general idea

28:21.566 --> 28:25.606 align:middle
or orientation towards the idea
of, of coming into the market

28:25.606 --> 28:28.806 align:middle
and affecting a particular
tenor of the bond market?

28:28.956 --> 28:29.946 align:middle
Do-do you like that idea?

28:29.946 --> 28:30.366 align:middle
Do you not like it?

28:30.366 --> 28:32.496 align:middle
Is it at the top of your
toolbox, or is it something

28:32.496 --> 28:34.366 align:middle
that you think is at the
bottom of the toolbox?

28:34.816 --> 28:35.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:35.146 --> 28:38.926 align:middle
Sure. So we monitor a broad
range of financial conditions,

28:39.006 --> 28:41.366 align:middle
and we're always attentive to
market developments, of course.

28:41.706 --> 28:44.466 align:middle
We're still a long way from
our goals, and it's important

28:44.466 --> 28:46.446 align:middle
that financial conditions
do remain accommodative

28:46.616 --> 28:48.126 align:middle
to support the achievement
of those goals.

28:48.596 --> 28:51.716 align:middle
And if you look at various
indexes of financial conditions,

28:51.716 --> 28:54.526 align:middle
what you'll see is that they
generally do show financial

28:54.526 --> 28:56.716 align:middle
conditions overall to
be highly accommodative.

28:57.046 --> 28:58.026 align:middle
And that is appropriate.

28:58.546 --> 29:01.386 align:middle
So that's, that's
how we look at it.

29:01.526 --> 29:04.506 align:middle
I would add, as I've
said, I would be concerned

29:04.506 --> 29:06.276 align:middle
by disorderly conditions
in markets

29:06.276 --> 29:08.656 align:middle
or by a persistent tightening
of financial conditions

29:08.936 --> 29:11.986 align:middle
that threaten the
achievement of our goals.

29:12.546 --> 29:14.816 align:middle
We think the stance of
our-of monetary policy

29:14.816 --> 29:15.716 align:middle
remains appropriate.

29:16.126 --> 29:17.926 align:middle
Our guidance on the
federal funds rate

29:17.926 --> 29:20.306 align:middle
and on asset purchases is
providing strong support

29:20.306 --> 29:20.956 align:middle
for the economy.

29:21.756 --> 29:23.476 align:middle
And we're committed
to maintaining

29:23.476 --> 29:25.016 align:middle
that patiently accommodative
stance

29:25.016 --> 29:27.096 align:middle
until the job is
well and truly done.

29:29.196 --> 29:30.076 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

29:30.206 --> 29:33.036 align:middle
Could, could you give us an
idea of how you sort of feel

29:33.036 --> 29:35.826 align:middle
about that tool, of being able
to come into a particular part

29:35.826 --> 29:38.186 align:middle
of the market and either
operating-doing an Operation

29:38.186 --> 29:39.346 align:middle
Twist or something like that?

29:39.346 --> 29:41.806 align:middle
Is that something you feel
to be the top of your toolbox

29:41.806 --> 29:43.856 align:middle
or something that you
don't really prefer?

29:44.456 --> 29:45.056 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:45.056 --> 29:47.386 align:middle
You know, we-the tools we
have are the tools we have.

29:47.386 --> 29:48.776 align:middle
What I'm telling you
is that the stance

29:48.776 --> 29:50.276 align:middle
of monetary policy
we have today,

29:50.276 --> 29:51.436 align:middle
we believe, is appropriate.

29:51.826 --> 29:53.846 align:middle
We think that our
asset purchases

29:54.106 --> 29:57.126 align:middle
in their current form-which
is to say, you know,

29:57.126 --> 30:00.156 align:middle
across the curve, $80
billion in Treasuries,

30:00.156 --> 30:03.236 align:middle
$40 billion in mortgage-backed
securities on net-we think

30:03.236 --> 30:06.286 align:middle
that that's, that's the right
place for our asset purchases.

30:06.286 --> 30:08.216 align:middle
Now, we can-we can change
them, of course, in,

30:08.646 --> 30:10.886 align:middle
in a number of different
dimensions should we deem

30:10.886 --> 30:11.866 align:middle
that that's appropriate.

30:11.916 --> 30:13.816 align:middle
But, for now, we think
that our policy stance

30:13.816 --> 30:14.646 align:middle
on that is appropriate.

30:14.646 --> 30:15.226 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

30:18.626 --> 30:19.716 align:middle
Thank you.

30:19.716 --> 30:20.546 align:middle
Rachel Siegel.

30:20.546 --> 30:24.396 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

30:24.396 --> 30:24.956 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

30:24.956 --> 30:26.446 align:middle
Thanks very much for
taking my question.

30:26.446 --> 30:30.056 align:middle
You've spoken about the
pandemic's disproportionate toll

30:30.056 --> 30:33.596 align:middle
on Black Americans, Hispanic
Americans, Asian Americans,

30:33.596 --> 30:36.226 align:middle
and other groups in
the labor market.

30:36.226 --> 30:38.796 align:middle
And I'm curious if you can
speak to specific indicators

30:38.796 --> 30:42.006 align:middle
that the Fed will be using to
measure job gains for groups

30:42.006 --> 30:43.586 align:middle
that have persistently
higher rates

30:43.586 --> 30:46.186 align:middle
of unemployment compared
to white Americans.

30:46.486 --> 30:49.736 align:middle
And, relatedly, since you've
described vaccines as key

30:49.736 --> 30:52.446 align:middle
to the economic recovery,
is the Fed concerned

30:52.446 --> 30:55.646 align:middle
about lower vaccination rates
in communities of color?

30:55.646 --> 30:57.946 align:middle
And what barriers do
you think exist there?

30:58.166 --> 30:59.056 align:middle
Thanks very much.

30:59.056 --> 30:59.966 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:59.966 --> 31:01.126 align:middle
So, which measures.

31:01.406 --> 31:07.196 align:middle
So we, we do monitor and
communicate very regularly

31:07.756 --> 31:11.646 align:middle
about different labor
market-disparities

31:11.646 --> 31:12.706 align:middle
in the labor market, let's say.

31:12.706 --> 31:15.326 align:middle
So the African American
unemployment rate is

31:15.466 --> 31:16.776 align:middle
substantially elevated,

31:17.296 --> 31:20.496 align:middle
and so is the Hispanic
unemployment rate.

31:20.496 --> 31:24.316 align:middle
So we look at those and we
see those as, you know-it's,

31:24.316 --> 31:25.696 align:middle
it's slack in the labor market.

31:25.696 --> 31:29.676 align:middle
It's, it's sad to see, because
those disparities had really

31:29.676 --> 31:32.456 align:middle
come down to record lows

31:32.456 --> 31:34.276 align:middle
since we started keeping
the data that way.

31:34.796 --> 31:37.686 align:middle
As recently as a year ago,
February of last year,

31:37.686 --> 31:39.546 align:middle
we had those, those
disparities quite low.

31:39.546 --> 31:42.236 align:middle
What happens in a downturn,
though, is they move

31:42.236 --> 31:45.386 align:middle
up at twice the speed
of white unemployment.

31:46.086 --> 31:47.826 align:middle
So we monitor those things.

31:47.886 --> 31:50.666 align:middle
We-our tools, of course,
affect unemployment generally,

31:50.666 --> 31:53.726 align:middle
but we're going to look at
those as, as a form of slack

31:53.726 --> 31:56.406 align:middle
in the labor market and
hope that, you know,

31:56.406 --> 31:57.396 align:middle
that there's progress there.

31:57.396 --> 31:59.756 align:middle
And, and this particular
downturn, of course,

31:59.756 --> 32:05.166 align:middle
was just a direct hit on,
on a part of the economy

32:05.166 --> 32:08.926 align:middle
that employs many minorities
and lower-paid workers.

32:08.926 --> 32:12.376 align:middle
The public-facing workers
in the service industries

32:13.076 --> 32:15.546 align:middle
in many cases don't have
a lot of financial assets,

32:15.546 --> 32:17.406 align:middle
they're not tremendously
well paid,

32:17.886 --> 32:19.816 align:middle
they might have other
jobs, and things like that.

32:19.816 --> 32:22.256 align:middle
So this was a direct hit on
that part of the economy.

32:22.256 --> 32:24.516 align:middle
And it's the slowest part
of the economy to recover.

32:24.516 --> 32:26.636 align:middle
So, you know, we'd like to
see those people continue

32:26.636 --> 32:28.596 align:middle
to get supported, you know,

32:28.596 --> 32:31.156 align:middle
as we-as the broader
economy recovers, which,

32:31.446 --> 32:32.696 align:middle
which it's very much doing now.

32:33.526 --> 32:35.556 align:middle
In terms of disparate
levels of vaccination,

32:35.786 --> 32:39.466 align:middle
that's-those are facts
and unfortunate facts.

32:39.466 --> 32:40.616 align:middle
They're really not something we,

32:40.646 --> 32:44.366 align:middle
we have within our
policy toolkit to address.

32:44.416 --> 32:47.986 align:middle
But it is-it is true, though,
that the data we have suggests

32:47.986 --> 32:49.796 align:middle
that there are-there are
significant disparities

32:49.796 --> 32:52.156 align:middle
between different ethnic
groups and-you know,

32:52.876 --> 32:54.356 align:middle
but that's, that's not for us.

32:54.416 --> 32:55.926 align:middle
That's for fiscal authorities

32:55.926 --> 32:58.266 align:middle
and the government more
generally to, to work on.

32:58.266 --> 32:58.906 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:01.266 --> 33:01.976 align:middle
Thank you.

33:01.976 --> 33:02.486 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek.

33:02.486 --> 33:03.156 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

33:03.156 --> 33:05.306 align:middle
Hey, Chair Powell.

33:05.546 --> 33:07.166 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

33:07.166 --> 33:10.096 align:middle
I was wondering if you
could talk a little bit

33:10.096 --> 33:14.826 align:middle
about how you see the fiscal
policy support that has come

33:14.826 --> 33:17.326 align:middle
down the line affecting
the economy's potential

33:17.326 --> 33:20.056 align:middle
in the longer term, just in the
sense that you've talked a lot

33:20.186 --> 33:22.436 align:middle
about the potential for
labor market scarring and how

33:22.436 --> 33:24.816 align:middle
that might weigh on our,
sort of, future prospects.

33:24.816 --> 33:27.496 align:middle
And I wonder whether you see
that sort of working in reverse.

33:27.496 --> 33:28.696 align:middle
You know, if we put people back

33:28.696 --> 33:30.136 align:middle
into the labor market
more quickly,

33:30.136 --> 33:33.106 align:middle
does that improve our chances?

33:33.426 --> 33:34.566 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:34.566 --> 33:40.996 align:middle
So I do think that fiscal policy
overall will have really helped

33:40.996 --> 33:44.916 align:middle
us to avoid much of the
scarring that we were very,

33:44.916 --> 33:46.366 align:middle
very concerned about
at the beginning.

33:46.896 --> 33:49.746 align:middle
And I think that's just
the size and the speed

33:49.746 --> 33:53.076 align:middle
with which Congress
has delivered-you know,

33:53.076 --> 33:57.636 align:middle
with the CARES Act and since
then, has-is going to wind

33:57.636 --> 34:01.396 align:middle
up very much accelerating the
return to full employment.

34:01.786 --> 34:04.056 align:middle
It's going to make a huge
difference in people's lives.

34:04.056 --> 34:04.996 align:middle
And it has already.

34:05.346 --> 34:07.826 align:middle
As I mentioned in my
remarks-opening remarks,

34:08.326 --> 34:10.716 align:middle
the recovery has been
faster than we expected.

34:11.016 --> 34:13.656 align:middle
Part of that just is,
it's very hard to predict,

34:13.656 --> 34:15.186 align:middle
given we've never seen
an event like this.

34:15.186 --> 34:17.806 align:middle
But part of it is just the
strength of the fiscal response,

34:18.276 --> 34:20.676 align:middle
which I think will, will
look good over the years.

34:21.316 --> 34:27.756 align:middle
Longer term, you know, to, to
really-that's-the first part

34:27.756 --> 34:29.836 align:middle
of it is about avoiding
scarring,

34:29.836 --> 34:32.066 align:middle
and I think we've not
avoided all of the scarring,

34:32.066 --> 34:34.356 align:middle
but we've probably avoided
the worst cases there.

34:34.356 --> 34:37.806 align:middle
And, and I hope we keep at
it, and we will keep at it

34:37.806 --> 34:39.766 align:middle
with our policies, of
course, to do whatever we can

34:39.766 --> 34:41.696 align:middle
to make sure that's-that
continues.

34:42.056 --> 34:45.096 align:middle
Longer term though,
what it takes

34:45.096 --> 34:49.436 align:middle
to drive productive capacity
per capita or per hour worked,

34:50.006 --> 34:52.966 align:middle
to raise living standards
over time, is investment:

34:52.966 --> 34:55.256 align:middle
investment in people's
skills and aptitudes,

34:55.256 --> 34:56.736 align:middle
investment in plant
and equipment,

34:56.736 --> 34:57.826 align:middle
investment in software.

34:57.826 --> 35:00.316 align:middle
It takes a lot of investment to,

35:00.546 --> 35:03.276 align:middle
to support a more
productive economy

35:03.276 --> 35:04.366 align:middle
and raise living standards.

35:04.366 --> 35:07.486 align:middle
And that's-you know, that
hasn't been the principal focus

35:07.486 --> 35:10.216 align:middle
with these measures-our
measures, certainly.

35:10.216 --> 35:11.386 align:middle
And we don't have those tools.

35:11.386 --> 35:14.216 align:middle
But what Congress has been doing
has mainly been replacing lost

35:14.216 --> 35:16.776 align:middle
income and beginning to,
you know, support people

35:16.776 --> 35:18.146 align:middle
as the economy returns
to normal.

35:18.146 --> 35:21.576 align:middle
But there should be a focus
on-a longer-term focus, I think,

35:21.576 --> 35:24.456 align:middle
would be healthy on, on
the investment front.

35:24.456 --> 35:25.066 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:27.076 --> 35:27.916 align:middle
Thank you.

35:27.916 --> 35:28.746 align:middle
James Politi.

35:28.746 --> 35:30.796 align:middle
JAMES POLITI.

35:30.796 --> 35:32.326 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

35:32.326 --> 35:35.856 align:middle
I wanted to ask a question
about the euro zone.

35:36.136 --> 35:39.446 align:middle
While the outlook for the U.S.
economy has much improved,

35:39.496 --> 35:42.906 align:middle
progress in the euro zone has
been far less encouraging.

35:42.906 --> 35:45.356 align:middle
And it's showing signs
of serious weakness due,

35:45.356 --> 35:48.396 align:middle
due to the slower
vaccination rollout

35:48.396 --> 35:51.556 align:middle
and renewed lockdowns
and restrictions.

35:51.556 --> 35:53.096 align:middle
How worried are you

35:53.096 --> 35:56.236 align:middle
about transatlantic
economic divergence

35:56.626 --> 36:00.596 align:middle
and the possibility that,
that trouble in the euro zone

36:00.596 --> 36:02.136 align:middle
and weakness in the
euro zone could,

36:02.226 --> 36:06.476 align:middle
could drag down the U.S.
recovery as it did, in a way,

36:06.476 --> 36:09.346 align:middle
in the aftermath of
the financial crisis?

36:09.346 --> 36:10.626 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:10.626 --> 36:13.276 align:middle
You're right that the
pace of the recovery is

36:13.776 --> 36:17.566 align:middle
that we're having
diverging recoveries here,

36:17.566 --> 36:19.176 align:middle
as we did after the last crisis.

36:19.176 --> 36:21.186 align:middle
And in this case, as
well as the other one,

36:21.186 --> 36:26.146 align:middle
the U.S.'s recovery is, is
leading the global recovery.

36:26.956 --> 36:32.566 align:middle
And, you know, we conduct
policy, of course, here.

36:33.186 --> 36:35.446 align:middle
Our, our focus is
on-our, you know-our,

36:35.446 --> 36:38.076 align:middle
our objectives are
domestic ones.

36:38.076 --> 36:39.246 align:middle
It's, it's maximum employment

36:39.246 --> 36:40.916 align:middle
and price stability here
in the United States.

36:41.476 --> 36:42.976 align:middle
We monitor developments abroad,

36:43.206 --> 36:45.226 align:middle
and we know-because
they know we know

36:45.226 --> 36:47.996 align:middle
that they can-those can
affect our outcomes.

36:48.686 --> 36:54.666 align:middle
So I think U.S. demand-very
strong U.S. demand, if,

36:55.646 --> 36:58.186 align:middle
if-as the economy
improves is going

36:58.186 --> 37:01.876 align:middle
to support global
activity as well over time.

37:02.386 --> 37:04.106 align:middle
That, that'll be
through imports.

37:04.206 --> 37:06.466 align:middle
And, you know, when the
U.S. economy is strong,

37:06.466 --> 37:09.476 align:middle
that strength tends to
be-tends to support global,

37:09.476 --> 37:10.986 align:middle
global activity as well.

37:11.446 --> 37:12.266 align:middle
So that's one thing.

37:12.726 --> 37:15.076 align:middle
I don't-I don't worry
in the near term.

37:15.076 --> 37:17.826 align:middle
I mean, I'd love to see
Europe growing faster.

37:17.826 --> 37:21.496 align:middle
I'd love to see the vaccine
rollout going more smoothly.

37:22.026 --> 37:25.946 align:middle
But I don't worry too much
about us in the near term,

37:25.946 --> 37:27.556 align:middle
because we're, we're
on a very good track.

37:27.556 --> 37:30.206 align:middle
Very strong fiscal
support coming,

37:30.306 --> 37:35.606 align:middle
now vaccination going
quickly, and cases coming down.

37:35.606 --> 37:37.416 align:middle
I think we're-I think
we're in a good place.

37:37.416 --> 37:40.036 align:middle
It's all ahead of us,
but the data should,

37:40.036 --> 37:42.516 align:middle
should get stronger
fairly quickly here

37:42.516 --> 37:44.166 align:middle
and remain strong
for some time here.

37:44.886 --> 37:45.666 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:47.106 --> 37:48.016 align:middle
Thank you.

37:48.016 --> 37:48.306 align:middle
Hannah Lang.

37:48.416 --> 37:49.316 align:middle
HANNAH LANG.

37:53.476 --> 37:56.146 align:middle
Hi, thanks so much for
taking our questions.

37:56.306 --> 37:58.216 align:middle
I wanted to ask if
the Fed is planning

37:58.216 --> 38:01.446 align:middle
on extending the same
restrictions on bank dividends

38:01.446 --> 38:04.406 align:middle
and share repurchases-I'm
sorry, share repurchases

38:04.406 --> 38:07.456 align:middle
that are currently in place
into the second quarter,

38:07.456 --> 38:11.566 align:middle
and if you're considering
at all the scenario analysis

38:11.566 --> 38:13.836 align:middle
and midcycle stress
tests that were

38:13.836 --> 38:15.306 align:middle
in place last year, this year.

38:15.306 --> 38:17.906 align:middle
And, kind of, what would make
you consider doing something

38:17.906 --> 38:18.706 align:middle
like that again?

38:19.706 --> 38:19.916 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:19.916 --> 38:21.476 align:middle
So we haven't made
a decision on that.

38:21.476 --> 38:24.396 align:middle
We're a couple weeks away
from announcing that decision.

38:24.396 --> 38:26.526 align:middle
I, I won't foreshadow
it here today.

38:27.046 --> 38:30.056 align:middle
I will say, we're going

38:30.056 --> 38:31.996 align:middle
to continue our data-driven
approach.

38:32.066 --> 38:34.276 align:middle
You know that we restricted
buybacks and dividends,

38:34.566 --> 38:36.206 align:middle
so the firms are
preserving capital.

38:36.666 --> 38:41.226 align:middle
Through 2020, the banks actually
increased their level of capital

38:41.646 --> 38:43.066 align:middle
and their level of reserves.

38:43.176 --> 38:46.846 align:middle
And the December stress tests
showed that banks are strong

38:46.846 --> 38:49.966 align:middle
and well capitalized under
our hypothetical recessions

38:50.546 --> 38:52.016 align:middle
that we-that we used
in December,

38:52.016 --> 38:53.066 align:middle
which were quite stringent.

38:53.496 --> 38:56.816 align:middle
We're right in the middle
of our 2021 stress tests,

38:56.816 --> 38:59.866 align:middle
and we'll release those
results before the end of June.

39:00.346 --> 39:03.266 align:middle
And that layers, you know, very
significant additional stress

39:03.666 --> 39:06.086 align:middle
on top of the stress the
banks have already absorbed

39:06.086 --> 39:08.876 align:middle
over the past year, with
the unemployment rate going

39:08.876 --> 39:12.066 align:middle
to 11 percent and stock prices
falling more than 50 percent.

39:12.066 --> 39:15.256 align:middle
So-but all of that, you know,
the results of the stress tests

39:15.256 --> 39:18.766 align:middle
and the decision on
distributions-all of that is

39:18.766 --> 39:22.966 align:middle
to come fairly soon,
as I mentioned.

39:22.966 --> 39:23.626 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:24.516 --> 39:24.916 align:middle
Thank you.

39:24.916 --> 39:26.516 align:middle
Edward Lawrence.

39:26.636 --> 39:30.606 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

39:30.606 --> 39:33.116 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell, for
taking these questions.

39:33.116 --> 39:34.976 align:middle
You laid out the
standards to lift off.

39:34.976 --> 39:37.596 align:middle
And back in June of
2020, you said, you know,

39:37.596 --> 39:39.736 align:middle
you're not even thinking about
thinking about raising rates.

39:39.736 --> 39:44.326 align:middle
But I see seven members seeing
liftoff in 2023 and 4 next year.

39:44.326 --> 39:47.286 align:middle
How, how much debate-and
how can you characterize

39:47.286 --> 39:48.876 align:middle
that conversation-has there been

39:48.876 --> 39:51.876 align:middle
about moving off the lower
bound earlier than signaled?

39:53.556 --> 39:54.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:55.096 --> 39:56.376 align:middle
Well, you know, it all depends.

39:56.376 --> 39:59.566 align:middle
We've set out very clear
criteria for liftoff, right,

39:59.566 --> 40:02.746 align:middle
where we've said we want to
see labor market conditions

40:02.746 --> 40:04.336 align:middle
that are consistent
with our estimates of,

40:04.796 --> 40:05.876 align:middle
of maximum employment.

40:06.006 --> 40:07.476 align:middle
And that doesn't just
mean unemployment,

40:07.476 --> 40:09.356 align:middle
it means a much broader
set of criteria.

40:09.736 --> 40:13.356 align:middle
We want inflation at 2 percent,
and not on a transitory basis.

40:13.356 --> 40:14.676 align:middle
And we want inflation on track

40:14.676 --> 40:18.196 align:middle
to be moderately-to
run moderately

40:18.196 --> 40:19.206 align:middle
above 2 percent for some time.

40:19.256 --> 40:20.216 align:middle
Those are the three conditions.

40:20.216 --> 40:22.116 align:middle
Everybody on the Committee
agrees to that, right?

40:22.546 --> 40:25.406 align:middle
So it comes down to, what's
your projection for the economy?

40:25.796 --> 40:27.436 align:middle
If you want to-if
you want to-you know,

40:27.436 --> 40:30.186 align:middle
people will have a
range of, of assessments

40:30.716 --> 40:32.246 align:middle
for how good the
economy is going to be.

40:32.246 --> 40:36.676 align:middle
And, you know, we, we
don't-I would say that we're

40:36.706 --> 40:39.646 align:middle
in a relatively highly
uncertain situation.

40:39.646 --> 40:42.426 align:middle
If you look at the
uncertainty, people,

40:42.426 --> 40:44.926 align:middle
people on the Committee
broadly say that uncertainty

40:44.926 --> 40:47.166 align:middle
about the forecast is,
is very high compared

40:47.166 --> 40:48.066 align:middle
to the normal level.

40:48.126 --> 40:49.796 align:middle
We haven't come out
of a pandemic before.

40:49.976 --> 40:50.866 align:middle
We haven't had this kind

40:50.866 --> 40:54.006 align:middle
of fiscal support before,
totaling it all up.

40:54.086 --> 40:56.716 align:middle
So you're going to have
different perspectives

40:56.716 --> 40:59.216 align:middle
from Committee participants
about how fast growth will be,

40:59.216 --> 41:02.886 align:middle
how fast the labor market
will heal, or how fast-sorry,

41:02.886 --> 41:03.906 align:middle
inflation will move up.

41:04.286 --> 41:07.556 align:middle
And those things are going
to dictate where people write

41:07.556 --> 41:09.506 align:middle
down an estimate of liftoff.

41:09.506 --> 41:12.226 align:middle
Of course, this isn't a
decision to lift off now.

41:12.496 --> 41:14.196 align:middle
We'll make that decision then.

41:14.586 --> 41:16.896 align:middle
But it's an estimate
based on-based

41:16.896 --> 41:18.356 align:middle
on assumptions about growth.

41:18.356 --> 41:19.996 align:middle
And it's, it's meant
to be a tool

41:19.996 --> 41:23.416 align:middle
to generally show
the public how,

41:23.416 --> 41:25.516 align:middle
how our objective
function works,

41:25.516 --> 41:26.796 align:middle
how we think about the future.

41:26.796 --> 41:30.206 align:middle
It isn't meant to actually
pin down a time when we might

41:30.206 --> 41:30.986 align:middle
or might not lift off.

41:31.356 --> 41:32.646 align:middle
We-you know, we're
not going to make

41:32.646 --> 41:33.966 align:middle
that decision for some time.

41:34.376 --> 41:36.556 align:middle
The chances are that
the economy in that time

41:36.556 --> 41:39.766 align:middle
and place will be very different
from the one we think it'll be.

41:39.866 --> 41:45.126 align:middle
So I-sometimes with the dots,
I have to be sure to point

41:45.216 --> 41:47.946 align:middle
out that they-they're
not a Committee forecast.

41:48.046 --> 41:48.906 align:middle
And you know this,

41:48.906 --> 41:50.506 align:middle
but it's-they're not
a Committee forecast.

41:50.506 --> 41:53.476 align:middle
It's just compiling
these projections,

41:53.476 --> 41:54.666 align:middle
really, of individual people.

41:55.136 --> 41:56.466 align:middle
We think it serves
a useful purpose.

41:56.466 --> 41:59.586 align:middle
It's not meant to, to
actually be a promise

41:59.586 --> 42:01.746 align:middle
or even a prediction of
when the Committee will act.

42:01.786 --> 42:05.516 align:middle
That will be very much
dependent on economic outcomes,

42:05.996 --> 42:07.376 align:middle
which are highly uncertain.

42:07.376 --> 42:08.026 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:10.566 --> 42:10.986 align:middle
Thank you.

42:10.986 --> 42:12.376 align:middle
We'll go to Brian Cheung.

42:12.376 --> 42:13.216 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

42:13.366 --> 42:14.616 align:middle
Hey there, Chairman Powell.

42:14.616 --> 42:16.926 align:middle
I wanted to elaborate a
little bit on your commentary

42:16.926 --> 42:18.796 align:middle
about the fiscal stimulus.

42:18.796 --> 42:22.986 align:middle
So it sounds like you,
I guess, see the case

42:22.986 --> 42:25.376 align:middle
for even maybe more investment,
at least from Congress,

42:25.376 --> 42:26.936 align:middle
to support the more
productive economy,

42:26.936 --> 42:28.466 align:middle
as you answered to
Jeanna's question.

42:28.466 --> 42:31.356 align:middle
So we just had $1.9
trillion dollars in spending.

42:31.356 --> 42:33.766 align:middle
So where do you see the
fiscal space right now?

42:33.966 --> 42:38.206 align:middle
Do you still, I guess, maybe see
the country in a place right now

42:38.206 --> 42:40.066 align:middle
where it wouldn't be a
concern if there were

42:40.066 --> 42:41.196 align:middle
to be more spending
at this time?

42:41.946 --> 42:42.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:42.186 --> 42:44.456 align:middle
So, Brian, it's not up
to us to decide what,

42:44.456 --> 42:46.506 align:middle
what Congress should
spend money on or when.

42:46.946 --> 42:49.436 align:middle
I was answering Jeanna's
question, which really was,

42:49.856 --> 42:54.966 align:middle
how do we assure lack of damage
to-scarring, for example,

42:54.966 --> 42:55.796 align:middle
or lack of damage

42:55.796 --> 42:57.456 align:middle
to the productive
capacity of the economy?

42:57.686 --> 42:58.686 align:middle
And I think what's happened

42:58.686 --> 43:01.396 align:middle
so far has done a
pretty good job of that.

43:02.156 --> 43:04.456 align:middle
But, really, I wanted to
make the longer-run point

43:04.456 --> 43:07.296 align:middle
that if-to work on the
productivity-on productivity

43:07.296 --> 43:10.676 align:middle
over longer periods,
that is-that is-comes

43:10.676 --> 43:12.206 align:middle
down to a number of things.

43:12.206 --> 43:13.636 align:middle
But one of those
things is investment:

43:13.636 --> 43:16.956 align:middle
investment in people, in
their skills, education,

43:17.096 --> 43:18.496 align:middle
aptitude, all of those things.

43:18.866 --> 43:20.496 align:middle
I'm not in any way suggesting

43:20.496 --> 43:23.326 align:middle
that that's something Congress
should work on right now.

43:23.446 --> 43:26.096 align:middle
Or that, you know, that's just
not my-that's not our job.

43:26.096 --> 43:31.286 align:middle
I'm just saying, that, that is
what fiscal policy can do that,

43:31.416 --> 43:33.936 align:middle
really, monetary
policy can't do-is,

43:33.936 --> 43:36.766 align:middle
is invest in the future
productive capacity

43:36.766 --> 43:38.876 align:middle
of the economy, raise
potential growth.

43:38.876 --> 43:41.936 align:middle
Those things are completely
tools that Congress has.

43:41.936 --> 43:44.086 align:middle
And, again, I wasn't
making a comment at all

43:44.086 --> 43:46.106 align:middle
on the current fiscal setting.

43:47.326 --> 43:48.846 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

43:50.516 --> 43:51.346 align:middle
Thank you.

43:51.346 --> 43:52.896 align:middle
Next to Michael Derby.

43:53.226 --> 43:53.576 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

43:56.066 --> 43:58.266 align:middle
Thank you for taking
my question.

43:58.266 --> 44:01.346 align:middle
I just wanted to get your-an
updated view on your sense

44:01.346 --> 44:05.186 align:middle
of your view on financial
stability risks and whether

44:05.186 --> 44:09.516 align:middle
or not you see any pockets of
excess out in financial markets

44:09.516 --> 44:12.516 align:middle
that concern you, either, you
know, specifically to that,

44:12.516 --> 44:14.756 align:middle
that area of the market
or as in terms of, like,

44:14.756 --> 44:16.806 align:middle
the threat that it
could pose to-propose

44:16.806 --> 44:18.366 align:middle
to the overall economy.

44:19.496 --> 44:19.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:20.026 --> 44:23.056 align:middle
Sure. So, as you know,
financial stability

44:23.056 --> 44:25.316 align:middle
for us is, is a framework.

44:25.316 --> 44:26.336 align:middle
It's not one thing.

44:26.336 --> 44:28.596 align:middle
It's not a particular
market or a particular asset

44:28.596 --> 44:29.316 align:middle
or anything like that.

44:29.316 --> 44:31.246 align:middle
It's a framework
that we, we have.

44:31.246 --> 44:34.746 align:middle
We report on it semiannually,
the Board gets a report

44:34.746 --> 44:37.656 align:middle
on it quarterly, and
we monitor every day.

44:37.656 --> 44:39.246 align:middle
And it has-it has four pillars,

44:39.246 --> 44:41.106 align:middle
and those are four
key vulnerabilities:

44:41.406 --> 44:45.166 align:middle
asset valuations, debt owed
by businesses and households,

44:45.496 --> 44:48.656 align:middle
funding risk, and leverage
among financial institutions.

44:48.656 --> 44:49.456 align:middle
Those four things.

44:49.456 --> 44:50.846 align:middle
And I'll just quickly
touch on them.

44:51.096 --> 44:52.706 align:middle
So if you look at
asset valuations,

44:53.926 --> 44:56.136 align:middle
you can say that
by some measures,

44:56.136 --> 45:00.296 align:middle
some asset valuations are
elevated compared to history.

45:00.386 --> 45:01.856 align:middle
I think that's clear.

45:01.966 --> 45:04.086 align:middle
In terms of households
and businesses,

45:04.636 --> 45:07.796 align:middle
households entered the, the
crisis in very good shape

45:07.836 --> 45:09.046 align:middle
by historical standards.

45:09.546 --> 45:12.266 align:middle
Leverage in the household
sector had been just kind

45:12.266 --> 45:14.236 align:middle
of gradually moving
down and down and down

45:14.236 --> 45:16.236 align:middle
since the-since the
financial crisis.

45:16.676 --> 45:19.396 align:middle
Now, there was-there was some
negative effects on that,

45:19.396 --> 45:21.146 align:middle
people lost their jobs
and that sort of thing,

45:21.146 --> 45:23.336 align:middle
but they've also gotten
a lot of support now.

45:23.336 --> 45:26.706 align:middle
So the damage hasn't been
as, as bad as we thought.

45:27.136 --> 45:31.416 align:middle
Businesses, by the same token,
had a high debt load coming in.

45:31.856 --> 45:34.596 align:middle
And-but-and many saw
their revenues decline.

45:34.596 --> 45:37.226 align:middle
But there's-they've done so much
financing, and there's a lot

45:37.226 --> 45:38.386 align:middle
of cash on their balance sheet.

45:38.386 --> 45:41.436 align:middle
So nothing in those two
sectors really jumps

45:41.436 --> 45:42.956 align:middle
out as really troubling.

45:43.366 --> 45:47.576 align:middle
Short term-I mentioned funding
risk as the-as the last one.

45:47.576 --> 45:53.126 align:middle
So we saw, again, in this
crisis, breakdowns in parts

45:53.126 --> 45:54.866 align:middle
of the short-term
funding markets-came

45:54.866 --> 45:56.376 align:middle
under a tremendous
amount of stress.

45:56.946 --> 45:59.706 align:middle
And they've been quiet
since the spring.

45:59.706 --> 46:01.726 align:middle
And, you know, we shut down
our facilities and all of that.

46:01.726 --> 46:08.536 align:middle
But we, we don't-we don't feel
like we can let the moment pass

46:08.536 --> 46:12.246 align:middle
without just saying again that
we-that those-some aspects

46:12.246 --> 46:14.986 align:middle
of the short-term funding
markets and, more broadly,

46:14.986 --> 46:18.346 align:middle
nonbank financial intermediation
didn't hold up so well

46:18.346 --> 46:20.806 align:middle
under great stress-under
tremendous stress.

46:21.066 --> 46:22.446 align:middle
And we need to go
back and look at that.

46:22.446 --> 46:26.006 align:middle
So a very high priority
for us as regulators

46:26.006 --> 46:28.286 align:middle
and supervisors is going
to be to go back-and this,

46:28.286 --> 46:30.696 align:middle
this will involve all the
other regulatory agencies;

46:30.696 --> 46:32.906 align:middle
it does involve all of
them as well-and see

46:32.906 --> 46:34.376 align:middle
if we can strengthen
those things.

46:34.696 --> 46:39.256 align:middle
So that's, that's a-sort of
a broader, detailed look.

46:39.756 --> 46:40.786 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:42.786 --> 46:43.596 align:middle
Thank you.

46:43.596 --> 46:44.356 align:middle
Michael McKee.

46:44.356 --> 46:44.886 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

46:46.366 --> 46:49.206 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, can you help me
understand something about the,

46:49.316 --> 46:51.146 align:middle
the SEP and your forecasts?

46:51.176 --> 46:54.416 align:middle
The inflation that you're
talking about for this year,

46:54.416 --> 46:55.946 align:middle
you say, is "transitory."

46:56.486 --> 47:00.336 align:middle
Then, by 2023, you're
back down to 2.1 percent.

47:00.746 --> 47:05.626 align:middle
There's no forecast for
a rate increase for 2023.

47:06.216 --> 47:08.526 align:middle
If you get to 3½
percent unemployment

47:08.666 --> 47:11.886 align:middle
but inflation is only at 2 or
2.1 percent, are you willing

47:11.886 --> 47:15.346 align:middle
to leave rates at zero
going forward from there-or,

47:15.346 --> 47:16.986 align:middle
roughly zero going
forward from there?

47:16.986 --> 47:20.586 align:middle
In other words, could we be
in a Japan-like situation

47:20.586 --> 47:24.856 align:middle
where rates just stay low
because inflation does?

47:24.896 --> 47:25.246 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:25.456 --> 47:28.546 align:middle
Well, again, I wouldn't read
too much into, into the,

47:28.686 --> 47:32.296 align:middle
the March 2021 SEP dot plot.

47:33.016 --> 47:35.796 align:middle
Remember what it is:
It's a compilation

47:35.796 --> 47:38.326 align:middle
of individual projections
by individual members.

47:38.956 --> 47:40.616 align:middle
They're all making
different assessments.

47:40.616 --> 47:45.156 align:middle
They, they have different
forecasts, economic forecasts.

47:45.156 --> 47:48.336 align:middle
Some have more optimistic
ones, some less optimistic.

47:48.986 --> 47:50.606 align:middle
And they're-also remember

47:50.606 --> 47:52.986 align:middle
that the SEP doesn't actually
include all the things that go

47:52.986 --> 47:54.026 align:middle
into maximum employment.

47:54.596 --> 47:57.566 align:middle
Right? It's, it's only-it
only includes unemployment.

47:57.566 --> 48:04.376 align:middle
So I, I would just say,
we've set out clear guidance.

48:04.376 --> 48:07.526 align:middle
The-the message from the SEP
that I would like to leave

48:07.526 --> 48:09.776 align:middle
with people is, we set
out clear guidance.

48:10.086 --> 48:12.346 align:middle
I mentioned what it was.

48:12.346 --> 48:14.396 align:middle
It's inflation up
to-no, sorry, it's,

48:14.396 --> 48:16.986 align:middle
it's labor market conditions
consistent with our estimates

48:16.986 --> 48:19.856 align:middle
of maximum employment-and
that's not just unemployment,

48:19.856 --> 48:22.976 align:middle
it's all the other
indicators, but, overall,

48:22.976 --> 48:25.066 align:middle
totaling up to maximum
employment; it's inflation

48:25.216 --> 48:28.016 align:middle
at 2 percent, and not on a
transient basis; and inflation

48:28.016 --> 48:31.586 align:middle
on track to exceed 2 percent
for-moderately for some time.

48:32.236 --> 48:33.156 align:middle
Those are the criteria.

48:33.566 --> 48:37.216 align:middle
We're committed to robustly
implementing that guidance.

48:37.856 --> 48:41.016 align:middle
And that's what this-that's
what this says.

48:41.606 --> 48:42.786 align:middle
That's really all it says.

48:42.956 --> 48:47.066 align:middle
We're going to wait until
those requirements are met.

48:47.066 --> 48:49.266 align:middle
And, again, you know, the, the
state of the economy in two

48:49.266 --> 48:51.896 align:middle
or three years is
highly uncertain.

48:52.456 --> 48:55.596 align:middle
And I wouldn't want to focus
too much on the exact timing

48:55.596 --> 48:57.786 align:middle
of a potential rate increase
that far into the future.

48:58.826 --> 48:59.286 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

48:59.286 --> 49:03.596 align:middle
If I could follow up, I'm
just wondering-before 2019,

49:03.786 --> 49:06.706 align:middle
I would say you were
focused on the problems

49:06.706 --> 49:08.266 align:middle
with having interest
rates too low.

49:09.006 --> 49:11.966 align:middle
Now are you saying we're
willing to live with it

49:12.036 --> 49:17.456 align:middle
until we reach these goals
even if you get-meet your goal

49:17.456 --> 49:19.326 align:middle
on maximum employment?

49:19.726 --> 49:21.016 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

49:21.016 --> 49:23.306 align:middle
So what I would say
is, we're committed

49:23.306 --> 49:26.116 align:middle
to giving the economy the
support that it needs to return

49:26.436 --> 49:29.366 align:middle
as quickly as possible
to a state

49:29.366 --> 49:31.136 align:middle
of maximum employment
and price stability.

49:31.716 --> 49:38.966 align:middle
And, you know, to the extent
having rates low and support

49:38.966 --> 49:42.796 align:middle
for monetary policy
broadly, to the extent

49:42.936 --> 49:46.426 align:middle
that raises other questions, we
think it's absolutely essential

49:47.016 --> 49:49.696 align:middle
to maintain the strength
and stability

49:49.696 --> 49:51.206 align:middle
of the-of the broader
financial system

49:52.196 --> 49:56.006 align:middle
and to carefully monitor
financial stability questions

49:56.006 --> 49:58.046 align:middle
if that's what you're
getting at.

49:58.046 --> 49:59.376 align:middle
You know, we, we do that.

49:59.376 --> 50:00.876 align:middle
We will-we monitor
them very carefully.

50:00.876 --> 50:05.096 align:middle
I, I would point out that, over
the long expansion-the longest

50:05.096 --> 50:06.626 align:middle
in U.S. history, 10 years

50:06.626 --> 50:11.086 align:middle
and 8 months-rates were very
low for-they were at zero for,

50:11.696 --> 50:14.406 align:middle
you know, 7 years and
then-and then never got above,

50:14.566 --> 50:17.266 align:middle
you know, 2.4 percent, roughly.

50:17.756 --> 50:21.086 align:middle
During that, we didn't
see, actually,

50:21.436 --> 50:23.166 align:middle
excess buildup of debt.

50:23.296 --> 50:26.316 align:middle
We didn't see asset
prices form into bubbles

50:26.316 --> 50:28.396 align:middle
that would threaten the
progress of the economy.

50:28.676 --> 50:31.876 align:middle
We didn't see the things you-we
didn't see a housing bubble.

50:31.876 --> 50:35.306 align:middle
You know, the things that have
tended to really hurt an economy

50:35.306 --> 50:37.146 align:middle
and have in recent
history hurt the U.S.,

50:37.146 --> 50:40.306 align:middle
we didn't see them build
up despite very low rates.

50:40.656 --> 50:43.546 align:middle
Part of that just is that you're
in a low-rate environment,

50:43.836 --> 50:45.946 align:middle
you're in a much lower rate
environment, and the connection

50:45.946 --> 50:48.956 align:middle
between low rates and the kind

50:48.956 --> 50:51.776 align:middle
of financial instability
issues is just not as tight

50:51.776 --> 50:53.296 align:middle
as people think it is.

50:53.456 --> 50:54.756 align:middle
That's not to say we ignore it.

50:54.756 --> 50:55.596 align:middle
We don't ignore it.

50:55.596 --> 50:57.046 align:middle
We, we watch it very carefully.

50:57.176 --> 50:59.046 align:middle
And we don't think-we think
there is a connection.

50:59.756 --> 51:02.676 align:middle
We say there is, but
it's not quite so clear.

51:03.146 --> 51:07.546 align:middle
We actually monitor
financial conditions very,

51:07.546 --> 51:08.716 align:middle
very broadly and carefully.

51:08.716 --> 51:11.826 align:middle
And we didn't do that before
the Global Financial Crisis 12

51:11.826 --> 51:12.156 align:middle
years ago.

51:12.156 --> 51:13.056 align:middle
Now we do.

51:13.566 --> 51:16.016 align:middle
And we've also, you know,
put a lot of time and effort

51:16.016 --> 51:20.596 align:middle
into strengthening the
large financial institutions

51:20.596 --> 51:21.806 align:middle
that form the core

51:21.976 --> 51:25.676 align:middle
of our financial system-are much
stronger, much more resilient.

51:26.156 --> 51:27.236 align:middle
That's true of the banks.

51:27.286 --> 51:28.576 align:middle
I think it's true of the CCPs.

51:28.576 --> 51:30.536 align:middle
We want it to be true of,

51:30.536 --> 51:35.246 align:middle
of other nonbank financial
intermediation, markets,

51:35.246 --> 51:36.326 align:middle
and, and institutions.

51:36.756 --> 51:41.626 align:middle
So I think that's, that's-you
know, monetary policy should be,

51:41.626 --> 51:47.386 align:middle
to me, for, for achieving
our macroeconomic aims.

51:47.766 --> 51:51.146 align:middle
Financial regulatory policy
and supervision should be

51:51.146 --> 51:54.896 align:middle
for strengthening the financial
system so that it is strong

51:54.896 --> 51:57.496 align:middle
and robust and can
withstand the kinds of things

51:57.496 --> 52:02.106 align:middle
that it couldn't, frankly-and we
learned that in 2008, '09, '10.

52:02.606 --> 52:04.966 align:middle
This time around,
the regulated part

52:05.276 --> 52:07.956 align:middle
of the financial system
held up very well.

52:08.516 --> 52:11.286 align:middle
We, we found some other areas
that need strengthening,

52:11.286 --> 52:12.366 align:middle
and that's what we're
working on now.

52:12.796 --> 52:13.786 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

52:15.586 --> 52:16.516 align:middle
Thank you.

52:16.516 --> 52:18.286 align:middle
Anneken, CNN.

52:18.406 --> 52:19.786 align:middle
ANNEKEN TAPPE.

52:20.766 --> 52:22.356 align:middle
Thanks for taking my question.

52:22.776 --> 52:25.196 align:middle
Chairman Powell, could you
talk to us a little bit

52:25.196 --> 52:28.516 align:middle
about the relationship
between the persistent pandemic

52:28.516 --> 52:32.026 align:middle
unemployment and the expected
increase in, in inflation?

52:32.226 --> 52:34.816 align:middle
Does the former offset
the latter to some degree?

52:35.256 --> 52:35.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

52:35.506 --> 52:36.666 align:middle
I'm sorry, could
you repeat that?

52:36.666 --> 52:37.756 align:middle
You broke up there for a second.

52:37.756 --> 52:38.516 align:middle
ANNEKEN TAPPE.

52:38.516 --> 52:40.606 align:middle
Oh, no, I was hoping
that wouldn't happen.

52:40.606 --> 52:41.136 align:middle
Can you hear me now?

52:41.136 --> 52:41.376 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

52:41.796 --> 52:44.166 align:middle
Yes, yes. ANNEKEN TAPPE.

52:44.166 --> 52:44.636 align:middle
Okay, great.

52:44.636 --> 52:47.556 align:middle
I was asking if you could
talk to us a little bit

52:47.556 --> 52:50.256 align:middle
about the relationship
between the persistent pandemic

52:50.256 --> 52:53.406 align:middle
unemployment and the expected
increase in inflation.

52:53.406 --> 52:56.126 align:middle
I'm wondering whether the
former offsets the latter and to

52:56.126 --> 52:58.816 align:middle
which degree you guys
are monitoring this.

52:58.846 --> 52:59.786 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

52:59.786 --> 53:01.266 align:middle
You're asking about
the relationship

53:01.266 --> 53:03.296 align:middle
between unemployment
and inflation?

53:04.516 --> 53:05.656 align:middle
Is that-is that the question?

53:06.096 --> 53:07.096 align:middle
ANNEKEN TAPPE.

53:07.096 --> 53:07.916 align:middle
Yes, exactly.

53:08.256 --> 53:09.226 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

53:09.496 --> 53:11.686 align:middle
Okay. ANNEKEN TAPPE.

53:11.686 --> 53:13.446 align:middle
If they offset each
other-the persistent

53:15.296 --> 53:16.296 align:middle
pandemic unemployment.

53:16.526 --> 53:17.546 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

53:17.546 --> 53:19.296 align:middle
Yes, I think I'm
hearing you correctly.

53:19.296 --> 53:21.206 align:middle
So I would say a couple things.

53:21.336 --> 53:24.406 align:middle
There was a time when there
was a tight connection

53:24.406 --> 53:26.136 align:middle
between unemployment
and inflation.

53:26.136 --> 53:27.546 align:middle
That time is long gone.

53:27.976 --> 53:31.256 align:middle
We had extremely
low unemployment in,

53:31.386 --> 53:32.506 align:middle
in-not extremely low.

53:32.506 --> 53:37.496 align:middle
We had low unemployment in 2018
and '19 and the beginning of '20

53:37.496 --> 53:39.426 align:middle
without having troubling
inflation at all.

53:39.786 --> 53:41.186 align:middle
We were at 3.5 percent.

53:41.186 --> 53:43.936 align:middle
We were bouncing around with
unemployment 3.5 percent

53:43.936 --> 53:44.626 align:middle
to 4 percent.

53:45.026 --> 53:46.256 align:middle
And it wasn't just unemployment.

53:46.296 --> 53:48.466 align:middle
Participation was high,
wages were moving up.

53:48.466 --> 53:50.036 align:middle
It was a very healthy thing.

53:50.666 --> 53:53.536 align:middle
And we didn't see price
inflation move up.

53:53.856 --> 53:59.266 align:middle
There is a relationship between
wage inflation and unemployment.

53:59.966 --> 54:04.966 align:middle
But that has not-what, what
happens is that when wages move

54:04.966 --> 54:08.536 align:middle
up because unemployment is low,
companies have been absorbing

54:08.536 --> 54:11.486 align:middle
that increase into their margins
rather than raising prices.

54:11.626 --> 54:14.226 align:middle
And that seems to be a
feature of late-cycle behavior.

54:14.716 --> 54:18.956 align:middle
So we're, we're not-when,
when we-when we seek

54:18.956 --> 54:22.746 align:middle
to achieve low unemployment,
high levels of employment,

54:23.376 --> 54:27.446 align:middle
which is our mandate, you know,
we think we have the freedom

54:27.446 --> 54:29.056 align:middle
to do that based on the data

54:29.636 --> 54:31.586 align:middle
without worrying too
much about inflation.

54:31.586 --> 54:32.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

54:35.176 --> 54:35.916 align:middle
Thank you.

54:35.916 --> 54:37.076 align:middle
Going to Greg Robb.

54:37.076 --> 54:37.386 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

54:41.426 --> 54:42.856 align:middle
Hi. Thanks for taking
my question.

54:42.856 --> 54:45.896 align:middle
I wanted to follow
up a little bit.

54:45.896 --> 54:48.086 align:middle
I, I understand what you're
saying about the dot plot and,

54:48.646 --> 54:50.756 align:middle
and the message you're trying
to send with the dot plot.

54:50.756 --> 54:54.376 align:middle
But does the same apply
for the taper tantrum?

54:54.376 --> 54:57.006 align:middle
You've said that it's
going to take "some time"

54:57.386 --> 55:01.936 align:middle
to get some fundamental progress
on your goals, but it seems like

55:02.026 --> 55:04.656 align:middle
"some time" could
now be any time.

55:05.036 --> 55:06.856 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

55:06.996 --> 55:10.356 align:middle
So we've said "substantial
further progress."

55:10.706 --> 55:15.486 align:middle
Now, if you go back, October,
November-sorry, November,

55:15.486 --> 55:18.106 align:middle
December, and January, progress

55:18.106 --> 55:21.996 align:middle
in the labor market
slowed very sharply.

55:21.996 --> 55:25.916 align:middle
So you had an average of
29,000 jobs per month.

55:26.026 --> 55:27.426 align:middle
If you go back and look at the,

55:27.426 --> 55:30.396 align:middle
the level of job
creation before that,

55:30.396 --> 55:32.446 align:middle
it was very, very
high-very high.

55:33.116 --> 55:35.606 align:middle
So we weren't making any
progress on the labor market

55:35.606 --> 55:38.266 align:middle
from November through January.

55:38.416 --> 55:41.346 align:middle
February, we saw a
nice-a nice pickup,

55:41.346 --> 55:45.846 align:middle
a good jobs report-379,465
private sector.

55:46.106 --> 55:47.026 align:middle
So that's good.

55:47.916 --> 55:49.426 align:middle
It can go so much
higher, though.

55:49.426 --> 55:50.926 align:middle
And, and, you know,
it would be nice

55:50.926 --> 55:53.566 align:middle
to see-to really make faster
progress that's different

55:53.566 --> 55:54.646 align:middle
from substantial progress,

55:54.646 --> 55:57.236 align:middle
we'd like to see it be,
be higher than that.

55:57.236 --> 55:58.006 align:middle
And I think it will be.

55:58.006 --> 56:01.176 align:middle
That's-the expectation is,
you'll see now, I think,

56:01.176 --> 56:03.856 align:middle
really strong job creation
return-not as high as it was

56:03.856 --> 56:06.986 align:middle
in the very early days of
the recovery, the reopening

56:06.986 --> 56:10.016 align:middle
of the economy, but,
nonetheless, very strong.

56:10.316 --> 56:12.816 align:middle
Okay. So what, what
I'm saying is,

56:13.226 --> 56:16.186 align:middle
to achieve substantial
progress from where we are,

56:16.186 --> 56:18.566 align:middle
having had three months of,
of very little progress,

56:19.026 --> 56:20.216 align:middle
is going to take some time.

56:20.516 --> 56:23.786 align:middle
And it's-we don't want to
get into-I don't want to get

56:23.786 --> 56:28.076 align:middle
into trying to put a pin
on the calendar someplace,

56:28.076 --> 56:30.146 align:middle
because it's going
to be data dependent.

56:30.806 --> 56:33.396 align:middle
When we see-when we
see ourselves on track

56:33.396 --> 56:35.486 align:middle
to make substantial
further progress,

56:35.996 --> 56:37.436 align:middle
we're going to-we're
going to say so.

56:37.436 --> 56:40.796 align:middle
We understand fully
that that test is one

56:40.796 --> 56:42.136 align:middle
that involves judgment.

56:42.436 --> 56:45.216 align:middle
If you remember, during
the Global Financial Crisis

56:45.216 --> 56:49.616 align:middle
recovery, we said quantitative
easing, number three,

56:49.616 --> 56:52.276 align:middle
was-the test was a
substantial improvement

56:52.276 --> 56:53.726 align:middle
in the outlook for
the labor market.

56:54.026 --> 56:54.706 align:middle
Well, what does that mean?

56:54.706 --> 56:56.856 align:middle
Well, it means a
substantial improvement

56:56.856 --> 56:57.856 align:middle
in the outlook for
the labor market.

56:57.856 --> 56:59.876 align:middle
It meant we would communicate
when we thought we had that.

57:00.086 --> 57:01.876 align:middle
This is just like
that, in a way.

57:01.876 --> 57:02.836 align:middle
What we're saying is,

57:02.836 --> 57:05.436 align:middle
substantial further
progress toward our goals.

57:05.826 --> 57:09.676 align:middle
We'll tell people when we
think-until we-until we

57:09.676 --> 57:13.646 align:middle
say-until we give a signal, you
can assume we're not there yet.

57:14.206 --> 57:17.046 align:middle
And as we approach it,
well in advance-well

57:17.046 --> 57:21.486 align:middle
in advance-we will give a signal
that, yes, we're on a path to,

57:21.486 --> 57:24.926 align:middle
to possibly achieve that,
to consider tapering.

57:24.926 --> 57:26.516 align:middle
So that's, that's how we're
planning to handle it.

57:26.516 --> 57:30.236 align:middle
It's not different,
really, from, from QE3.

57:30.756 --> 57:33.016 align:middle
And I think we've
learned-what we've learned

57:33.056 --> 57:35.676 align:middle
from the experience of
these last dozen years is

57:35.676 --> 57:39.666 align:middle
to communicate very carefully,
very clearly, well in advance,

57:40.156 --> 57:43.006 align:middle
and then follow through with,
with your communications.

57:43.376 --> 57:46.226 align:middle
In this case, it's, it's an
outcome-based set of guidance,

57:46.856 --> 57:48.846 align:middle
as our rate guidance is,
and it's going to depend

57:48.936 --> 57:49.966 align:middle
on the progress of the economy.

57:49.966 --> 57:52.476 align:middle
That's why it's not appropriate
to start pointing at dates yet.

57:52.866 --> 57:53.966 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

57:54.246 --> 57:55.196 align:middle
Thank you.

57:55.196 --> 57:55.976 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

57:55.976 --> 57:56.806 align:middle
Thank you.

57:56.806 --> 57:58.456 align:middle
Don Lee, L.A. Times.

58:01.346 --> 58:04.036 align:middle
DON LEE. Hi, Chair Powell.

58:04.326 --> 58:07.326 align:middle
As you know, households,
households are sitting

58:07.326 --> 58:10.146 align:middle
on a lot of excess savings.

58:10.146 --> 58:14.666 align:middle
And I wonder if, combined with
that, you have an unleashing of,

58:15.356 --> 58:16.646 align:middle
you know, pent-up demand.

58:16.716 --> 58:19.706 align:middle
How much do you think that
would affect-that would

58:19.706 --> 58:21.166 align:middle
affect inflation?

58:21.166 --> 58:24.056 align:middle
And would you expect
that to be transitory?

58:24.316 --> 58:25.966 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

58:25.966 --> 58:29.366 align:middle
We, you know, we and everyone
who's forecasting these-what,

58:29.366 --> 58:32.126 align:middle
what we're all doing
is, we are looking

58:32.126 --> 58:33.496 align:middle
at the-at the amount of savings.

58:33.496 --> 58:35.496 align:middle
We're looking at-you know,

58:35.496 --> 58:38.826 align:middle
we have reasonably
good data on that.

58:38.826 --> 58:39.886 align:middle
And we're looking

58:39.886 --> 58:42.276 align:middle
at the government
transfers that'll be made

58:42.276 --> 58:43.706 align:middle
as part of the various laws.

58:44.226 --> 58:46.466 align:middle
And we're trying to
make an assessment

58:47.166 --> 58:51.006 align:middle
on what will be the tendency
of people to spend that money,

58:51.006 --> 58:52.606 align:middle
the marginal propensity
to consume.

58:53.366 --> 58:58.626 align:middle
And from that, you can develop
an estimate of the impact

58:58.626 --> 59:02.766 align:middle
on spending, on growth,
on hiring, and,

59:02.766 --> 59:04.186 align:middle
ultimately, on inflation.

59:04.246 --> 59:05.586 align:middle
So that's, that's
what we're all doing.

59:05.586 --> 59:09.656 align:middle
And we have, you know, we
have-we can look at history,

59:09.656 --> 59:10.726 align:middle
and we can make estimates,

59:10.726 --> 59:12.886 align:middle
and those are all very
transparent and public.

59:12.886 --> 59:14.206 align:middle
And you can compare
one to the other.

59:14.206 --> 59:16.176 align:middle
And, of course, we've
all-we've all done that.

59:16.176 --> 59:18.416 align:middle
And I think we've made very
conservative assumptions

59:18.996 --> 59:21.216 align:middle
and sensible mainstream
assumptions

59:21.216 --> 59:23.596 align:middle
at each step of that process.

59:23.596 --> 59:26.566 align:middle
And what, what it comes down to
is what I said before, which is,

59:27.096 --> 59:32.726 align:middle
there very likely will be a
step-up in inflation as March

59:32.726 --> 59:35.266 align:middle
and April of last year drop
out of the 12-month window,

59:35.266 --> 59:37.036 align:middle
because they were very
low inflation numbers.

59:37.446 --> 59:39.876 align:middle
That's a-that'll be a fairly
significant pop in inflation.

59:39.876 --> 59:41.136 align:middle
It'll wear off quickly, though,

59:41.136 --> 59:43.756 align:middle
because it-just the way
the numbers are calculated.

59:44.906 --> 59:48.166 align:middle
Past that, as the
economy reopens,

59:48.776 --> 59:52.066 align:middle
people will start spending
more, and then, you know,

59:52.066 --> 59:54.356 align:middle
you can only go out to
dinner once per night,

59:54.726 --> 59:56.266 align:middle
but a lot of people
can go out to dinner.

59:56.266 --> 59:58.476 align:middle
And so-and they're
not doing that now.

59:58.476 --> 59:59.596 align:middle
They're not going
to restaurants,

59:59.596 --> 01:00:00.396 align:middle
not going to theaters.

01:00:00.396 --> 01:00:03.276 align:middle
That part of the economy-and
travel and hotels-that part

01:00:03.276 --> 01:00:06.686 align:middle
of the economy is, is
really not functioning at,

01:00:06.686 --> 01:00:07.716 align:middle
at full capacity, right?

01:00:07.716 --> 01:00:10.296 align:middle
But as that happens,
people can start to spend.

01:00:10.906 --> 01:00:13.446 align:middle
It's also-wouldn't be surprising
if-and you're seeing this now,

01:00:13.446 --> 01:00:15.776 align:middle
particularly in the goods
economy-there'll be bottlenecks.

01:00:15.966 --> 01:00:19.536 align:middle
They won't be-they won't
be able to service all

01:00:19.536 --> 01:00:21.526 align:middle
of the demand maybe,
maybe for a period.

01:00:21.736 --> 01:00:23.746 align:middle
So those things could
lead to-and we've,

01:00:23.746 --> 01:00:24.446 align:middle
we've modeled that.

01:00:24.446 --> 01:00:25.226 align:middle
Other people have too.

01:00:25.226 --> 01:00:29.016 align:middle
We've-and what we see is
relatively modest increases

01:00:29.016 --> 01:00:29.606 align:middle
in inflation.

01:00:30.466 --> 01:00:33.126 align:middle
So-but those are not
permanent things.

01:00:33.126 --> 01:00:35.536 align:middle
You know, what'll, what'll
happen is-the supply side-the

01:00:35.536 --> 01:00:37.756 align:middle
supply side in the United
States is very dynamic.

01:00:37.756 --> 01:00:41.636 align:middle
People start businesses,
they, they reopen restaurants,

01:00:41.636 --> 01:00:45.176 align:middle
you know, the airlines
are, will be flying again,

01:00:45.176 --> 01:00:46.566 align:middle
all of those things will happen.

01:00:46.896 --> 01:00:51.456 align:middle
And so it'll turn out to be a
one-a one-time sort of bulge in,

01:00:51.586 --> 01:00:56.236 align:middle
in prices, but it won't change
inflation going forward,

01:00:56.236 --> 01:00:59.016 align:middle
because inflation expectations
are strongly anchored

01:00:59.406 --> 01:01:00.476 align:middle
around 2 percent.

01:01:01.156 --> 01:01:03.746 align:middle
We know that inflation
dynamics do evolve over time.

01:01:03.906 --> 01:01:05.286 align:middle
And there was a time when,

01:01:05.486 --> 01:01:07.846 align:middle
when inflation went
up, it would stay up.

01:01:08.306 --> 01:01:09.966 align:middle
And, and that time is not now.

01:01:09.966 --> 01:01:11.806 align:middle
That hasn't been the
case for some decades.

01:01:12.176 --> 01:01:15.376 align:middle
And we think it won't-it
won't-we won't suddenly change

01:01:15.376 --> 01:01:16.326 align:middle
to another regime.

01:01:16.326 --> 01:01:17.976 align:middle
These things tend
to change over time.

01:01:18.726 --> 01:01:20.086 align:middle
And they tend to-tend to change

01:01:20.086 --> 01:01:22.816 align:middle
when the central bank
doesn't understand

01:01:23.096 --> 01:01:25.816 align:middle
that having inflation
expectations anchored

01:01:25.816 --> 01:01:27.716 align:middle
at 2 percent is the
key to it all.

01:01:28.226 --> 01:01:29.716 align:middle
The, the-having them anchored

01:01:29.716 --> 01:01:33.316 align:middle
at 2 percent is what gives
us the ability to push hard

01:01:33.816 --> 01:01:35.296 align:middle
when the economy's really weak.

01:01:35.746 --> 01:01:39.316 align:middle
If we saw inflation
expectations moving materially

01:01:39.316 --> 01:01:42.096 align:middle
above 2 percent, of course, we
would conduct policy in a way

01:01:42.096 --> 01:01:44.466 align:middle
that would, would make sure
that that didn't happen.

01:01:44.466 --> 01:01:46.806 align:middle
We're committed to having
inflation expectations anchored

01:01:46.896 --> 01:01:51.066 align:middle
at 2 percent, not materially
above or below 2 percent.

01:01:51.476 --> 01:01:54.236 align:middle
So that's-I think if you-if
you look at the savings,

01:01:54.236 --> 01:01:56.506 align:middle
look at all of that,
model it, that's,

01:01:56.506 --> 01:01:59.546 align:middle
that's kind of what comes
out of our assessment.

01:02:00.226 --> 01:02:01.656 align:middle
There are different
possibilities.

01:02:01.656 --> 01:02:05.166 align:middle
I think it's, it's a relatively
unusual-very unusual situation

01:02:05.166 --> 01:02:07.336 align:middle
to have all these
savings and this,

01:02:07.336 --> 01:02:10.276 align:middle
this amount of fiscal support
and monetary policy support.

01:02:10.996 --> 01:02:13.946 align:middle
Nonetheless, that, that
is our most likely case.

01:02:13.946 --> 01:02:16.426 align:middle
And you know, as
the-as the data come in

01:02:16.426 --> 01:02:18.796 align:middle
and the economy performs,
we'll of course adjust.

01:02:19.136 --> 01:02:22.306 align:middle
Our outcome-based guidance will
immediately adapt, we think,

01:02:22.766 --> 01:02:25.466 align:middle
to meet whatever the actual
path of the economy is.

01:02:25.466 --> 01:02:26.306 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

01:02:28.836 --> 01:02:29.316 align:middle
Thank you.

01:02:29.316 --> 01:02:31.976 align:middle
We'll go to Scott Horsley
for the last question.

01:02:35.546 --> 01:02:36.136 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

01:02:36.136 --> 01:02:37.376 align:middle
Thanks, Chairman Powell.

01:02:37.776 --> 01:02:40.046 align:middle
My question is about those
supply chain bottlenecks,

01:02:40.046 --> 01:02:41.676 align:middle
especially on the goods side.

01:02:42.286 --> 01:02:46.166 align:middle
Are they getting better,
or are they getting worse?

01:02:46.166 --> 01:02:48.306 align:middle
We saw they were sort of a drag

01:02:48.306 --> 01:02:50.476 align:middle
on the industrial production
numbers we-that came

01:02:50.476 --> 01:02:51.276 align:middle
out yesterday.

01:02:51.736 --> 01:02:53.916 align:middle
And what do you expect
them to do to prices in,

01:02:53.916 --> 01:02:55.876 align:middle
in-just in the short run?

01:02:56.016 --> 01:02:56.356 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

01:02:56.356 --> 01:03:00.076 align:middle
You know, I have to say, I think
it's impossible to say, frankly,

01:03:00.076 --> 01:03:01.136 align:middle
with any confidence, but I,

01:03:01.166 --> 01:03:03.436 align:middle
I would expect it's very
possible-let's put it

01:03:03.436 --> 01:03:06.786 align:middle
that way-that you will
see bottlenecks emerge

01:03:06.786 --> 01:03:08.616 align:middle
and then clear over time.

01:03:08.616 --> 01:03:10.766 align:middle
And you'll probably see that
over a period of time, because,

01:03:11.326 --> 01:03:16.386 align:middle
you know, really, the, the
strong data are ahead of us.

01:03:16.506 --> 01:03:18.166 align:middle
You know, you're,
you're-right now the,

01:03:18.166 --> 01:03:22.256 align:middle
the checks are going
out just, just now.

01:03:22.716 --> 01:03:25.536 align:middle
And that'll, that'll
add to spending.

01:03:26.816 --> 01:03:28.276 align:middle
COVID cases are coming down.

01:03:28.276 --> 01:03:30.486 align:middle
Vaccination is moving,
now, quickly.

01:03:31.126 --> 01:03:33.746 align:middle
You know, the, the really
strong economic data is coming.

01:03:33.876 --> 01:03:37.556 align:middle
It should be coming, assuming
we stay on this track.

01:03:38.026 --> 01:03:39.626 align:middle
And that's when you'll
really know where,

01:03:39.626 --> 01:03:40.736 align:middle
where the bottlenecks are.

01:03:40.856 --> 01:03:42.196 align:middle
But you can see, though,

01:03:42.196 --> 01:03:43.906 align:middle
that they're not-these
are not permanent.

01:03:44.316 --> 01:03:46.506 align:middle
It's not like the supply
side will be unable

01:03:46.506 --> 01:03:47.576 align:middle
to adapt to these things.

01:03:47.576 --> 01:03:48.946 align:middle
It will-the market will clear.

01:03:49.546 --> 01:03:50.886 align:middle
It just may take some time.

01:03:50.886 --> 01:03:53.566 align:middle
And, and some of the
answer to that may be price.

01:03:53.796 --> 01:03:55.376 align:middle
In many cases, it
won't be price.

01:03:56.106 --> 01:03:59.426 align:middle
You'll see that people are
reluctant to raise prices.

01:03:59.596 --> 01:04:02.666 align:middle
You know, it's a little bit
the story about, you know,

01:04:02.666 --> 01:04:05.386 align:middle
the wage-the wage
Phillips curve does show

01:04:05.386 --> 01:04:07.546 align:middle
that as unemployment
goes down, wages move up.

01:04:07.986 --> 01:04:10.586 align:middle
But companies choose not
to try to, to pass that,

01:04:10.696 --> 01:04:13.516 align:middle
that price increase
along to their customers.

01:04:13.986 --> 01:04:15.886 align:middle
So you'll see a lot of
that here too, I think.

01:04:16.346 --> 01:04:17.306 align:middle
Anyway, we'll, we'll see.

01:04:17.306 --> 01:04:20.196 align:middle
But that's, that's my
basic, basic sense.

01:04:21.156 --> 01:04:22.196 align:middle
Thank you very much.

01:04:23.506 --> 01:04:34.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

01:04:34.616 --> 01:04:41.506 align:middle
Thank you.

